window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["rag-llamaindex"]=[{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0001","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-001-1-rag-overview-use-cases","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s002","q":"What is Retrieval Augmented Generation, and what problem does it solve?","a":"<p>RAG connects an LLM to an external data store. At question time it retrieves relevant text, adds it to the prompt, and lets the LLM answer from that text. It solves four LLM limits: stale knowledge (training data is a static snapshot), no source attribution, hallucination, and generic answers with no private or domain data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0002","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-001-1-rag-overview-use-cases","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s002","q":"Walk through the three steps of a RAG request in order.","a":"<p>Retrieve, augment, generate. First the query is used to fetch relevant chunks from an external store. Then the chunks and the query are put into one prompt. Then the LLM writes the answer from that prompt. The order matters because the LLM can only use context that was already placed in its prompt.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0003","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-001-1-rag-overview-use-cases","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s002","q":"Why does RAG reduce hallucination, and why does it not remove it completely?","a":"<p>The model is given real text to answer from, and that text can be updated or deleted at any time without retraining. It is not removed because retrieval can return wrong or irrelevant chunks, and the LLM can still ignore the context or fill gaps from its own weights. Faithfulness checks and good prompts are still needed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0004","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-001-1-rag-overview-use-cases","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s002","q":"RAG or fine-tuning: how do you choose when an LLM must answer questions about company documents that change weekly?","a":"<p>Use RAG. Knowledge that changes often is cheaper to update in a store than to retrain into weights, RAG can point to the exact passages used, and access control can be applied per document. Fine-tuning fits changing the style, format or behaviour of the model, or teaching a stable skill. The two can be combined.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0005","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-001-1-rag-overview-use-cases","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s002","q":"How does RAG give you source attribution when a plain LLM cannot?","a":"<p>The answer is built from specific retrieved chunks, so the system can return those chunks and their metadata (file name, page) next to the answer. A plain LLM has its knowledge mixed into its weights and cannot say where a claim came from. In LlamaIndex a query response exposes the retrieved chunks as <code>response.source_nodes</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0006","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-001-1-rag-overview-use-cases","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s002","q":"In LlamaIndex, which three lines make the smallest working RAG app over a folder of files?","a":"<p>Load, index, query:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>documents = SimpleDirectoryReader(&quot;./data&quot;).load_data()\nindex = VectorStoreIndex.from_documents(documents)\nresponse = index.as_query_engine().query(&quot;What are the key themes?&quot;)</code></pre></div>\n<p>Loading maps to the retrieve side, <code>from_documents</code> chunks and embeds the text into an in-memory vector index, and the query engine retrieves top chunks and sends them to the LLM. Defaults are used for the LLM and embedding model, which in LlamaIndex 0.10+ is an OpenAI model unless you set <code>Settings</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from llama_index.core import VectorStoreIndex, SimpleDirectoryReader\n...</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0007","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-002-use-cases","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s003","q":"Name the typical production use cases for RAG and what they have in common.","a":"<p>Question answering over private documents, customer support bots, medical and legal research assistants, and conversational search with citations. They share two needs: answers must rest on current, private or specialised data the base model never saw, and users want to verify where the answer came from.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0008","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-002-use-cases","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s003","q":"How would you make a LlamaIndex RAG app show citations to the user?","a":"<p>Use a query engine that tracks its retrieved chunks, for example <code>CitationQueryEngine.from_args(index, citation_chunk_size=512)</code>, which numbers the retrieved chunks and has the LLM cite them. You can also print <code>response.source_nodes</code> with each node's metadata such as file name and page. Smaller citation chunks give more precise citations but need more context slots.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0009","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-002-use-cases","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s003","q":"Why is RAG a good fit for legal or medical research but needs extra care there?","a":"<p>These fields need current, citable material (statutes, case law, guidelines) that changes and is too large for the context window. The care: a wrong or fabricated answer has high cost, so you need faithfulness checks, source display, and a path for the model to say it does not know.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0010","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-003-environment","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s004","q":"Why keep API keys in a <code>.env</code> file loaded with <code>python-dotenv</code>, and what must you do with that file in git?","a":"<p>Keys stay out of source code and notebooks, and each environment can use its own values. <code>load_dotenv()</code> copies the file's values into environment variables, which you read with <code>os.getenv</code>. Add <code>.env</code> to <code>.gitignore</code> and commit only a template with placeholder values, because a pushed key must be treated as leaked and revoked.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0011","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-003-environment","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s004","q":"Why pin library versions such as <code>llama-index==0.10.37</code>, and what is special about LlamaIndex in that regard?","a":"<p>LlamaIndex changes fast: class names, import paths and integration packages have moved between releases. Pinned versions give repeatable installs, so tutorial code and your code run the same. In LlamaIndex 0.10+ the core and each integration (LLM, embeddings, vector store) are separate pip packages with their own versions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0012","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-003-environment","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s004","q":"Why use a conda or virtual environment for a RAG project instead of installing into the system Python?","a":"<p>It isolates dependencies so different projects can use different LlamaIndex or client versions, makes the setup reproducible, and lets you register the environment as a Jupyter kernel so notebooks run against it. Installing globally leads to version conflicts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0013","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-003-environment","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s004","q":"What are the pros and cons of running a RAG project in a cloud dev environment such as GitHub Codespaces?","a":"<p>Pros: nothing to install, an identical environment for everyone, and a machine size you can pick. Cons: it uses cloud quota and needs internet, and secrets must be set as environment secrets rather than committed. Local runs are better for offline work or sensitive data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0014","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-004-choosing-an-llm-embeddings-provider","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s005","q":"In LlamaIndex 0.10+, how do you add a new LLM provider to a project, and why is it done that way?","a":"<p>Install that provider's integration package, for example <code>pip install llama-index-llms-openai</code>, then import the class from <code>llama_index.llms.&lt;provider&gt;</code> and create it with the model name and key. Integrations are separate packages so the core stays small and you install only what you use. All providers expose the same LLM interface, so switching is mostly a change of import and constructor.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0015","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-004-choosing-an-llm-embeddings-provider","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s005","q":"What criteria do you use to choose an LLM provider for a RAG application?","a":"<p>Fit to the use case, measured quality on your own questions, licence (commercial use, attribution), language support, security and privacy (encryption, data retention, GDPR or HIPAA needs), cost per token, latency, rate limits, and the strength of the community and support. Test two or three candidates on a small evaluation set of your own questions instead of trusting leaderboards.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0016","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-004-choosing-an-llm-embeddings-provider","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s005","q":"What is the difference between a provider that trains its own models and one that hosts open-source models, and when does each fit?","a":"<p>Providers such as OpenAI, Anthropic, Cohere and Google sell their own models behind an API. Hosts such as Together AI, Fireworks, Replicate and Hugging Face serve open-weight models for you. Hosted open models give more choice and often lower cost or easier licence terms. Own-model providers usually lead in top quality and features such as function calling.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0017","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-004-choosing-an-llm-embeddings-provider","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s005","q":"Why should the embedding model be chosen with the same care as the LLM, and what must stay consistent?","a":"<p>Embedding quality decides retrieval quality, and retrieval limits what the LLM can answer. A weak embedding model misses synonyms, domain terms or other languages, and no prompt or LLM can recover chunks that were never retrieved. Test candidate models on your own queries, for example with hit rate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0018","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-005-choosing-a-vector-database","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s006","q":"What criteria do you weigh when choosing a vector database for RAG?","a":"<p>Search speed and accuracy at your data size, horizontal scalability, metadata filtering and hybrid (keyword plus vector) search, support for real-time inserts and updates, persistence and backup, integration with your framework, ease of deployment (managed or self-hosted), cost and licence. Metadata filters and updates matter most in practice because RAG data changes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0019","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-005-choosing-a-vector-database","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s006","q":"Why do you need a vector database at all, instead of a plain list of vectors in memory?","a":"<p>It persists embeddings so you do not pay to re-embed on every start, it uses approximate nearest-neighbour indexes (such as HNSW) that stay fast at millions of vectors, and it supports metadata filters, updates, deletes and concurrent clients. The in-memory default of LlamaIndex is fine for prototypes only.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0020","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-005-choosing-a-vector-database","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s006","q":"In LlamaIndex, how do you connect to a Qdrant collection, and what two values come from your Qdrant cluster?","a":"<p>Create a <code>QdrantClient</code> with the cluster <code>url</code> and <code>api_key</code>, then wrap it in <code>QdrantVectorStore(client=client, collection_name=...)</code>. The URL and API key come from the cluster, and the key is shown only once at creation, so store it in a secrets file or environment variable straight away.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0021","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-005-choosing-a-vector-database","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s006","q":"What is the trade-off between a managed vector database and a self-hosted one?","a":"<p>Managed means no operations work, easy scaling and a free tier for prototypes, but recurring cost, data leaving your network, and rate or size limits. Self-hosted gives control over data location and cost at scale, but you handle upgrades, backups, scaling and security.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0022","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-007-3-llamaindex-fundamentals","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s008","q":"What is LlamaIndex, and which problem does it solve compared with calling an LLM API directly?","a":"<p>LlamaIndex is a data framework for building LLM apps over your own data. It gives ready parts for loading data, splitting it, embedding, indexing, storing, retrieving, querying, chatting and agents. Calling an LLM API directly leaves all of that plumbing to you.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0023","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-007-3-llamaindex-fundamentals","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s008","q":"In LlamaIndex 0.10+, what are the three kinds of packages, and why is it split that way?","a":"<p><code>llama-index-core</code> holds the framework (loading, indexing, querying, evaluation) with no third-party integrations. Integration packages add LLMs, embedding models, vector stores and readers, each installed separately. Llama Packs are community templates. The split keeps the core small and avoids installing heavy dependencies you do not use.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0024","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-007-3-llamaindex-fundamentals","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s008","q":"What do the building blocks of LlamaIndex (reader, index, retriever, query engine, chat engine, agent) each do, and how do they connect?","a":"<p>A reader loads data into documents. An index organises it for retrieval. A retriever fetches the relevant nodes. A query engine adds response synthesis to answer one question. A chat engine adds memory for multi-turn conversations. An agent can use tools, including query engines, to plan steps. Flow: data, reader, index, engine, LLM, response.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0025","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-007-3-llamaindex-fundamentals","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s008","q":"In LlamaIndex 0.10+, what does the global <code>Settings</code> object do, and what is the benefit and the risk?","a":"<p>It sets the default LLM (and similarly <code>Settings.embed_model</code>, <code>chunk_size</code> and others) used by every component that is not given one explicitly. The benefit is that you configure once. The risk is hidden global state: a component that silently falls back to the default can use a different model than you expect, so a query-time embedding model that differs from the indexing one gives bad retrieval.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from llama_index.core import Settings\nfrom llama_index.llms.openai import OpenAI\n\nSettings.llm = OpenAI(model=&quot;gpt-4o&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0026","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-007-3-llamaindex-fundamentals","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s008","q":"What is LlamaHub, and when would you use it?","a":"<p>LlamaHub is a catalogue of community and official integrations: data loaders, tools, LLMs, embedding models, vector stores and Llama Packs. Use it to find a reader for a data source (Notion, Slack, SQL) or a vector-store integration before writing your own.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0027","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-008-3-1-using-llms","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s009","q":"At which two stages of a LlamaIndex pipeline is an LLM used?","a":"<p>At indexing, for tasks such as summarising or extracting metadata from chunks. At query time, for retrieval decisions (choosing a source, using tools, rewriting the query) and for response synthesis, where the answer is written from the retrieved context.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0028","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-008-3-1-using-llms","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s009","q":"In LlamaIndex, what is the difference between <code>llm.complete</code>, <code>llm.chat</code> and <code>llm.stream_chat</code>?","a":"<p><code>complete(prompt)</code> takes one string and returns one completion. <code>chat(messages)</code> takes a list of <code>ChatMessage</code> objects with roles (system, user, assistant) and returns a reply, so history and a system prompt are explicit. <code>stream_chat(messages)</code> returns tokens as they are produced, so the user sees text early. Use chat for conversations and streaming for user-facing latency.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0029","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-008-3-1-using-llms","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s009","q":"What does this code print, and why is the loop written with <code>r.delta</code>?","a":"<p>It prints the reply token by token as one continuous text. Each item <code>r</code> carries the full text so far in <code>r.message</code> and only the new piece in <code>r.delta</code>. Printing <code>delta</code> avoids repeating earlier text, and <code>end=\"\"</code> stops a newline after each piece.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>response = llm.stream_chat(messages)\nfor r in response:\n    print(r.delta, end=&quot;&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0030","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-008-3-1-using-llms","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s009","q":"In LlamaIndex, why use a <code>PromptTemplate</code> with placeholders instead of building prompts with string concatenation?","a":"<p>A template keeps the fixed instruction text separate from the variables, is reusable, and can be listed and replaced on query engines through <code>get_prompts()</code> and <code>update_prompts()</code>. <code>template.format(thing=...)</code> fills the placeholders. Concatenation hides the structure and makes the prompt hard to swap out.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0031","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-008-3-1-using-llms","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s009","q":"In LlamaIndex, how do you give an LLM a system instruction, and why use <code>ChatMessage</code> roles?","a":"<p>Put the instruction in a message with the system role, followed by the user message, and call <code>llm.chat(messages)</code>. Roles tell chat-tuned models which text is a standing rule, which is user input, and which is a previous assistant reply, and they map to the provider's own message format.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from llama_index.core.llms import ChatMessage\n\nmessages = [\n    ChatMessage(role=&quot;system&quot;, content=&quot;You are a concise assistant.&quot;),\n    ChatMessage(role=&quot;user&quot;, content=&quot;Explain RAG.&quot;),\n]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0032","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-008-3-1-using-llms","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s009","q":"What does the <code>temperature</code> parameter of an LLM do, and what value suits a RAG answering step?","a":"<p>It controls randomness when picking the next token: low values give more deterministic output, high values more varied output. For RAG question answering you usually choose a low value (0 to 0.3) so answers stay close to the retrieved context and are repeatable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0033","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-008-3-1-using-llms","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s009","q":"In LlamaIndex, what is the difference between <code>SimpleChatEngine</code> and a chat engine made from an index?","a":"<p><code>SimpleChatEngine</code> only talks to the LLM with memory and has no retrieval, so it answers from the model's own knowledge. An engine made with <code>index.as_chat_engine()</code> retrieves from your data on each turn. Use the simple one for plain chat or testing the LLM, the indexed one for grounded answers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0034","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-009-3-2-loading-data","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s010","q":"What are the three stages of ingestion in LlamaIndex, and how do they compare to ETL?","a":"<p>Load (a reader makes documents), transform (split into nodes, add metadata, embed), index and store (write to a vector store). It resembles ETL: extract, transform, load. The quality of the transform step decides retrieval quality later.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0035","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-009-3-2-loading-data","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s010","q":"What does <code>SimpleDirectoryReader</code> do, and what does it return for a PDF?","a":"<p>It reads files from a folder or a list of files and picks a parser by file extension (text, markdown, PDF, Word, images and more). It returns a list of <code>Document</code> objects. For PDFs it returns one <code>Document</code> per page, with file name and page label in the metadata.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0036","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-009-3-2-loading-data","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s010","q":"What is a <code>Document</code> in LlamaIndex, and why attach metadata to it?","a":"<p>A <code>Document</code> is a container for text plus metadata and an id, produced by a reader. Metadata (file name, author, date, page) is copied to the nodes made from it. It can be used for filtering at query time, shown as a citation, and optionally included in what gets embedded or shown to the LLM.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0037","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-009-3-2-loading-data","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s010","q":"What is a node in LlamaIndex, and how is it different from a document?","a":"<p>A node is a chunk of a document: the unit that is embedded, stored and retrieved. It holds text, metadata, and relationships to other nodes and to its source document. A document is the whole source item. Retrieval returns nodes (with scores), not whole documents.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0038","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-009-3-2-loading-data","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s010","q":"In LlamaIndex, what do <code>chunk_size</code> and <code>chunk_overlap</code> mean for <code>SentenceSplitter</code>, and what happens if you change them?","a":"<p><code>chunk_size</code> is the maximum chunk length in tokens and <code>chunk_overlap</code> is how many tokens neighbouring chunks share. Smaller chunks give precise matches but lose context and produce more nodes to embed. Larger chunks keep context but blur the embedding and use more of the LLM context window. Overlap reduces the chance that an answer is cut at a boundary, at the cost of duplicate text.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from llama_index.core.node_parser import SentenceSplitter\n\nparser = SentenceSplitter(chunk_size=128, chunk_overlap=16)\nnodes = parser.get_nodes_from_documents(documents)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0039","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-009-3-2-loading-data","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s010","q":"Why does <code>SentenceSplitter</code> tend to give better chunks than cutting every N characters?","a":"<p>It tries to break at sentence and paragraph boundaries and only falls back to smaller units when needed, so a chunk rarely ends mid-sentence. Fixed-size cuts can split a sentence or a fact in two, which hurts the embedding and the answer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0040","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-009-3-2-loading-data","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s010","q":"In LlamaIndex, how are node relationships used, and when are they useful?","a":"<p>Relationships link nodes as next and previous (sequence) or parent and child (hierarchy). Each entry stores the id of the related node. They let a retriever fetch neighbours or a parent after a match, which suits books with chapters or legal text with clauses, and they power recursive and small-to-big retrieval.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from llama_index.core.schema import TextNode, NodeRelationship, RelatedNodeInfo\n\nnode1 = TextNode(text=&quot;first&quot;, id_=&quot;001&quot;)\nnode2 = TextNode(text=&quot;second&quot;, id_=&quot;002&quot;)\nnode2.relationships[NodeRelationship.PREVIOUS] = RelatedNodeInfo(node_id=node1.node_id)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0041","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-009-3-2-loading-data","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s010","q":"How would you create a <code>Document</code> by hand and attach metadata to it?","a":"<p>Pass <code>text</code> and a <code>metadata</code> dict to the constructor, for example <code>Document(text=\"...\", metadata={\"category\": \"legal\"})</code>. You can also assign <code>doc.metadata = {...}</code> later. Do this when the data does not come from a reader, such as rows from a database or API.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0042","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-010-3-3-indexing","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s011","q":"What is an embedding, and why is its length fixed?","a":"<p>An embedding is a vector of numbers that represents the meaning of a text, so similar texts are close in vector space. Its length is set by the model, not by the input: a one-word string and a long paragraph give vectors of the same size (for example 1024 for Cohere embed-english-v3.0, 1536 for OpenAI text-embedding-3-small). The fixed size is what makes comparing any two texts possible.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0043","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-010-3-3-indexing","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s011","q":"Why must a vector collection's dimension match the embedding model, and what breaks if you switch models later?","a":"<p>The collection stores vectors of one fixed size, and similarity only makes sense between vectors from the same model. A different model with another dimension is rejected by the store, and one with the same dimension still gives meaningless scores. You must re-embed all data into a new collection.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0044","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-010-3-3-indexing","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s011","q":"What does cosine similarity measure, and why is it common for text embeddings?","a":"<p>It measures the angle between two vectors, from -1 to 1, ignoring their length. Text embeddings encode meaning in direction, so the angle is the useful signal and length differences do not matter. Euclidean distance or dot product are alternatives, and many models are trained for one of them, so use the metric the model recommends.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0045","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-010-3-3-indexing","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s011","q":"In LlamaIndex, which methods embed a single string versus many strings, and why use the batch one?","a":"<p><code>get_text_embedding(text)</code> embeds one string and <code>get_text_embedding_batch(list)</code> embeds a list. The batch call sends several texts per API request, which cuts network overhead and request counts, and it is what indexing uses internally.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0046","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-010-3-3-indexing","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s011","q":"How would you use a free local embedding model with LlamaIndex, and what are the trade-offs?","a":"<p>Install <code>llama-index-embeddings-huggingface</code>, create the model as shown, and set it as <code>Settings.embed_model</code> or pass it to the index. It runs on your machine, so there is no per-token cost and data stays local. The trade-offs are your own compute (CPU is slow for big corpora) and possibly lower quality than top hosted models. FastEmbed offers lighter quantised models optimised for CPU.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from llama_index.embeddings.huggingface import HuggingFaceEmbedding\n\nembed_model = HuggingFaceEmbedding(model_name=&quot;BAAI/bge-small-en-v1.5&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0047","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-011-3-3-indexing","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s011","q":"In LlamaIndex, what is the difference between <code>VectorStoreIndex.from_documents</code>, <code>VectorStoreIndex(nodes)</code> and <code>VectorStoreIndex.from_vector_store</code>?","a":"<p><code>from_documents(docs)</code> splits the documents with the default node parser, embeds the nodes and stores them. <code>VectorStoreIndex(nodes)</code> builds from nodes you already made, giving control over chunking. <code>from_vector_store(store)</code> wraps a vector store that already holds embeddings and does not embed anything, which is how you reconnect to existing data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0048","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-011-3-3-indexing","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s011","q":"What does this code do, and what is wrong if <code>embed_model</code> is left out when the data was embedded with a non-default model?","a":"<p>It builds an index object over embeddings that already exist in the store, with no new embedding. The query is still embedded at search time with the configured embed model, which is the default unless you set <code>Settings.embed_model</code> or pass <code>embed_model=</code>. If that model differs from the one used for indexing, similarity scores are meaningless or the store raises a dimension error.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>index = VectorStoreIndex.from_vector_store(vector_store=vector_store)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0049","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-011-3-3-indexing","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s011","q":"In LlamaIndex, why does <code>VectorStoreIndex.from_documents</code> expect a list even for a single document?","a":"<p>It iterates over documents to parse and embed each one, so a bare <code>Document</code> fails. Wrap it as <code>[doc]</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0050","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-011-3-3-indexing","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s011","q":"Indexing a large corpus is slow and costs money. How do you keep that cost down while developing?","a":"<p>Index a small slice of the data while iterating, set <code>show_progress=True</code> to watch it, persist the index or use a vector store so you never re-embed unchanged data, and use an ingestion pipeline with caching. Embedding calls are billed per token, so repeated full re-indexing is the main avoidable cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0051","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-011-3-3-indexing","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s011","q":"When would you build <code>VectorStoreIndex(nodes)</code> from nodes instead of using <code>from_documents</code>?","a":"<p>When you need control that the default parser does not give: a specific splitter (semantic or hierarchical), custom metadata or relationships added to nodes, or nodes produced by an earlier ingestion pipeline. <code>from_documents</code> is shorter but hides these choices.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0052","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-012-3-4-storing","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s012","q":"Why persist an index, and what are the options in LlamaIndex?","a":"<p>By default the index lives in memory, so it is lost on exit and rebuilding it means paying to re-embed. Options: <code>index.persist()</code> writes the stores to disk (simple, one machine), or you use a vector database such as Qdrant, which also serves multiple clients and scales. Use disk for prototypes and a vector database for production.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0053","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-012-3-4-storing","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s012","q":"What is a <code>StorageContext</code> in LlamaIndex and which stores does it hold?","a":"<p>It tells LlamaIndex where each kind of data goes: <code>vector_store</code> for embeddings, <code>docstore</code> for nodes and documents, <code>index_store</code> for index metadata, and <code>graph_store</code> for knowledge graphs. You create it with <code>StorageContext.from_defaults(vector_store=...)</code> and pass it to the index.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0054","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-012-3-4-storing","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s012","q":"How do you reload a persisted LlamaIndex index from disk, and why must you set the same embed model?","a":"<p>Rebuild a <code>StorageContext</code> that points at the <code>persist_dir</code> written by <code>index.persist()</code>, then call <code>load_index_from_storage</code>. This reads the stored nodes and vectors without re-embedding. Queries are still embedded at search time, so <code>Settings.embed_model</code> (or the <code>embed_model</code> argument) must be the model used at indexing. Otherwise the query vector comes from a different model, and retrieval is wrong or fails on dimension.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>storage_context = StorageContext.from_defaults(persist_dir=&quot;./storage&quot;)\nindex = load_index_from_storage(storage_context)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0055","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-012-3-4-storing","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s012","q":"What are the steps to store a LlamaIndex index in Qdrant, and why does their order matter?","a":"<p>Create the client, then the vector store bound to a collection, then the storage context that holds the store, then build the index with that context. Each step needs the previous object, and if you skip <code>storage_context</code> the index silently uses an in-memory store instead of Qdrant.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>client = qdrant_client.QdrantClient(url=QDRANT_URL, api_key=QDRANT_API_KEY)\nvector_store = QdrantVectorStore(client=client, collection_name=&quot;docs&quot;)\nstorage_context = StorageContext.from_defaults(vector_store=vector_store)\nindex = VectorStoreIndex.from_documents(documents, storage_context=storage_context)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0056","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-012-3-4-storing","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s012","q":"With <code>QdrantVectorStore</code>, text is kept inside the vector store. What does <code>store_nodes_override=True</code> change when building an index?","a":"<p>Vector stores that keep the node text (flag <code>stores_text</code>) normally do not also write nodes to the docstore, to save space. <code>store_nodes_override=True</code> forces the nodes to be written to the docstore too, which you need for features that read the docstore, such as some retrievers and document management. The cost is extra storage.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0057","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-012-3-4-storing","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s012","q":"What is a Qdrant collection, and how should you organise data across collections?","a":"<p>A collection is a set of vectors of one dimension and distance metric, plus payload (metadata). Use one collection per embedding model and per distinct corpus or experiment, since each has a fixed vector size. To separate tenants within one collection, use metadata filters instead of creating many collections.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0058","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-013-3-5-retrieving","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s013","q":"How does a LlamaIndex retriever work, step by step?","a":"<p>The query text is embedded with the same model used at indexing, the vector store runs a similarity search, and the top-k nodes come back as <code>NodeWithScore</code> objects (node plus score). No LLM call happens, so no answer is written. You call it with <code>retriever.retrieve(query)</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0059","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-013-3-5-retrieving","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s013","q":"What is the difference between a retriever and a query engine in LlamaIndex?","a":"<p>A retriever returns raw nodes and scores. A query engine wraps a retriever, optional postprocessors and a response synthesizer, so it returns an LLM-written answer. Use a retriever to debug or evaluate retrieval, or when you want to handle the generation yourself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0060","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-013-3-5-retrieving","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s013","q":"What does <code>similarity_top_k</code> control, and what happens if it is too low or too high?","a":"<p>It sets how many nodes are returned. Too low can miss the needed chunk (low recall). Too high adds irrelevant chunks, which wastes context window, raises cost and latency, and can distract the LLM (low precision). Tune it with an evaluation set, and use reranking to fetch many and keep a few.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>retriever = index.as_retriever(similarity_top_k=5)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0061","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-013-3-5-retrieving","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s013","q":"When retrieval returns few or wrong results, how do you debug it in LlamaIndex?","a":"<p>Call <code>retriever.retrieve(query)</code> and print each node's score, id and text. Check that the right chunk exists in the index, that chunking did not split the answer, that the query and the documents use the same embedding model, and that <code>similarity_top_k</code> is big enough. Fix retrieval before touching the prompt or the LLM.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0062","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-013-3-5-retrieving","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s013","q":"Name some retriever types in LlamaIndex and why you would use a recursive one.","a":"<p>Vector retrievers (plain similarity search), fusion retrievers (combine results of several queries or retrievers), and recursive retrievers that follow node relationships, for example from a small matching chunk to its parent. Recursive retrieval helps when small chunks match well but the LLM needs the surrounding context.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0063","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-014-3-6-querying","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s014","q":"What three stages happen inside a LlamaIndex query engine call?","a":"<p>Retrieval (top-k nodes), postprocessing (rerank, filter or transform the nodes), then response synthesis (the query, the context and a prompt go to the LLM). Each stage can be customised on its own.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0064","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-014-3-6-querying","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s014","q":"How do you stream an answer from a LlamaIndex query engine, and how do you see which nodes backed it?","a":"<p>Create the engine with <code>streaming=True</code> and call <code>response.print_response_stream()</code> (or iterate <code>response.response_gen</code>) to show tokens as they arrive. The retrieved nodes are in <code>response.source_nodes</code>, each with its text, metadata and score, so you can show citations or check retrieval.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>query_engine = index.as_query_engine(streaming=True)\nresponse = query_engine.query(&quot;What is covered?&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0065","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-014-3-6-querying","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s014","q":"In LlamaIndex, what are the main <code>chat_mode</code> values of <code>as_chat_engine</code>, and how do <code>condense_question</code> and <code>context</code> differ?","a":"<p><code>simple</code> (no retrieval), <code>condense_question</code>, <code>context</code>, <code>condense_plus_context</code>, <code>react</code> and <code>best</code>. <code>condense_question</code> rewrites the history plus the new message into a standalone question and queries the index with it, so it always retrieves, but the answer does not see the raw history. <code>context</code> retrieves with the latest message and puts the context into the system prompt while keeping chat history, which is faster (one fewer LLM call) but can retrieve badly for follow-ups like \"why?\". <code>condense_plus_context</code> does both.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0066","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-014-3-6-querying","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s014","q":"Why does a chat engine need a query-rewriting step for follow-up questions?","a":"<p>A follow-up such as \"What about the second one?\" has no meaning alone, and an embedding of it will not match the right chunks. Rewriting it with the history into a standalone question gives the retriever text that carries the subject. The cost is one extra LLM call and the risk of a wrong rewrite.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0067","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-014-3-6-querying","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s014","q":"What does <code>chat_mode=\"best\"</code> do in LlamaIndex 0.10+?","a":"<p>It picks an engine based on the LLM: an OpenAI function-calling agent if the model supports function calling, otherwise a ReAct agent. It is the default mode of <code>as_chat_engine</code>. Because the choice depends on the model, behaviour can change when you swap LLMs, so set the mode explicitly in production.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0068","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-014-3-6-querying","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s014","q":"How do a query engine and a chat engine differ, and how do you choose?","a":"<p>A query engine answers one independent question and keeps no memory. A chat engine keeps conversation history so follow-ups work. Choose a query engine for one-shot lookup and API-style use, and a chat engine for assistants and support bots.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0069","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-014-3-6-querying","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s014","q":"In a LlamaIndex chat engine, what does <code>ChatMemoryBuffer</code> with <code>token_limit</code> do, and what happens when the history grows past it?","a":"<p><code>ChatMemoryBuffer</code> keeps the conversation history the chat engine sends to the LLM. When the history exceeds <code>token_limit</code>, the oldest messages are dropped so the prompt fits the context window. The trade-off is that the bot forgets early turns. Set the limit to leave room for retrieved context and the answer, or use a summarising memory for long chats.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>memory = ChatMemoryBuffer.from_defaults(token_limit=3000)\nchat_engine = index.as_chat_engine(chat_mode=&quot;context&quot;, memory=memory)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0070","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-015-3-6-querying","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s014","q":"Why would you build a <code>RetrieverQueryEngine</code> by hand instead of calling <code>index.as_query_engine()</code>?","a":"<p>To control each stage: which retriever and top-k, which postprocessors (similarity cutoff, reranker), and which response synthesizer. <code>as_query_engine()</code> builds a default version of this, so use the manual form when defaults do not fit, or when you want to swap one part without touching the others.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>query_engine = RetrieverQueryEngine(\n    retriever=retriever,\n    response_synthesizer=response_synthesizer,\n    node_postprocessors=[similarity_processor],\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0071","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-015-3-6-querying","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s014","q":"What happens if a similarity cutoff removes all retrieved nodes in a LlamaIndex query engine, and how should the app behave?","a":"<p>The synthesizer gets no context. In LlamaIndex 0.10+ the response is typically \"Empty Response\", rather than a grounded answer. The app should catch this and return a clear \"I do not know\" or ask the user to rephrase, instead of letting the LLM guess.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0072","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-015-3-6-querying","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s014","q":"Why fetch more nodes in the retriever than you finally send to the LLM?","a":"<p>A wide first pass (say top 10 to 20) gives high recall, then postprocessors such as a similarity cutoff or reranker keep the best few for precision. The LLM context is limited and extra chunks add noise, cost and latency.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0073","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-016-3-7-agents","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s015","q":"What is an agent in LlamaIndex, and how does it differ from a query engine?","a":"<p>An agent is an LLM that decides which tools to call, in what order and with what arguments, then uses the results to continue until it can answer. A query engine runs a fixed retrieve-then-synthesize flow. Agents handle multi-step tasks and several data stores, but cost more LLM calls and are less predictable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0074","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-016-3-7-agents","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s015","q":"What does ReAct mean, and what does the loop look like?","a":"<p>ReAct is Reasoning plus Acting. The LLM writes a thought, chooses an action (a tool call with arguments), receives the observation, and repeats until it can give a final answer. It works with any LLM that can follow the format, even without native function calling.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0075","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-016-3-7-agents","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s015","q":"In LlamaIndex, why do the docstring and type hints of a function matter when you wrap it as a tool?","a":"<p><code>FunctionTool.from_defaults</code> takes the name, description and argument schema from the function name, docstring and type hints, and the agent's LLM reads them to decide when and how to call the tool. A vague or missing docstring leads to wrong or missed tool calls, so write it like a prompt.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def multiply(a: int, b: int) -&gt; int:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Multiply two integers.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    return a * b\n\nmultiply_tool = FunctionTool.from_defaults(fn=multiply)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0076","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-016-3-7-agents","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s015","q":"In LlamaIndex 0.10+, how do you create a ReAct agent from tools, and what does <code>verbose=True</code> give you?","a":"<p><code>ReActAgent.from_tools(tools, llm=...)</code> builds the agent and <code>agent.chat(...)</code> runs the loop. <code>verbose=True</code> prints each thought, action and observation, which is the main way to debug why an agent chose a tool. Later LlamaIndex releases moved agents toward a workflow-based API, so check the docs for your version.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>agent = ReActAgent.from_tools([multiply_tool, add_tool], llm=llm, verbose=True)\nresponse = agent.chat(&quot;Multiply 3 by 4 and add 5&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0077","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-016-3-7-agents","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s015","q":"How do you check that an agent really used its tools instead of answering from the LLM's memory?","a":"<p>Make the tools return results the model could not guess, for example custom arithmetic rules, then compare the agent's answer with the value computed directly. Also read the verbose trace for tool calls. A correct answer to a normal question proves nothing, because the model may know it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0078","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-016-3-7-agents","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s015","q":"Why should you test several LLMs for an agent instead of assuming the biggest one is best?","a":"<p>Models differ in tool selection, argument formatting and following the ReAct format. A larger model can do worse on tool use than a smaller one from the same family. Run the same task set against each candidate and compare accuracy, cost and latency.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0079","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-016-3-7-agents","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s015","q":"What are the risks of agents compared with a fixed RAG pipeline?","a":"<p>More LLM calls (cost and latency), non-deterministic paths, possible loops or wrong tool arguments, and harder evaluation. Limit with a maximum number of steps, clear tool descriptions, logging, and by using a fixed pipeline whenever the task does not need planning.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0080","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-016-3-7-agents","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s015","q":"In LlamaIndex, how does a function-calling agent such as <code>OpenAIAgent</code> differ from a <code>ReActAgent</code>, and when do you choose each?","a":"<p>A function-calling agent uses the LLM's native tool-calling API, so the model returns structured tool names and arguments. This is more reliable and cheaper in tokens, but needs a model that supports function calling. <code>ReActAgent</code> makes the model write thoughts and actions as plain text, which works with any LLM, including local ones, but is more fragile because the text format can be broken. Choose function calling when the model supports it, ReAct otherwise. Class names and packages differ across LlamaIndex releases (0.10-0.11 shown), so check your version.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0081","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-017-4-1-components-and-subsystems","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s016","q":"What components make up a basic RAG system and what does each do?","a":"<p>LLM (writes the answer), prompt (query plus context template), document loader (reads raw data), chunker (splits into pieces), embedding model (turns chunks and queries into vectors), vector store (stores and searches vectors), retriever (fetches relevant chunks).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0082","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-017-4-1-components-and-subsystems","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s016","q":"What are the three subsystems of a RAG pipeline and what steps does each contain?","a":"<p>Indexing (load, chunk, embed, store), retrieval (embed the query with the same model, vector search, get chunks), and augment and generate (build the prompt with query and context, call the LLM). Indexing runs offline or on a schedule, the other two run on every query.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0083","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-017-4-1-components-and-subsystems","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s016","q":"Which parts of a RAG system run offline and which at query time, and why does that matter for design?","a":"<p>Loading, chunking, embedding and storing run offline (batch or scheduled), so they can be slow and cached. Query embedding, search, reranking and generation run per request, so they decide user latency. Put expensive work in indexing when possible, for example precomputed summaries.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0084","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-017-4-1-components-and-subsystems","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s016","q":"Where in a RAG pipeline would you look first when the system returns irrelevant answers?","a":"<p>At retrieval. Print the retrieved chunks for the query. If they are wrong, the fault is in chunking, embeddings, top-k or filters. If they are right but the answer is wrong, the fault is in the prompt or the LLM. Splitting the check this way avoids guessing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0085","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-018-4-2-ingestion-pipeline","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s017","q":"What is an <code>IngestionPipeline</code> in LlamaIndex, and why use it instead of calling the load, parse and index APIs separately?","a":"<p>It applies a list of transformations (splitters, metadata extractors, embedding model) to documents and returns nodes or writes them to a vector store. Compared with the low-level calls it needs less code and adds caching, deduplication and upserts. The cost is less fine control over each step.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0086","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-018-4-2-ingestion-pipeline","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s017","q":"How does caching work in a LlamaIndex ingestion pipeline?","a":"<p>Each pair of input node and transformation is hashed, and the output is cached under that hash. A later run with the same input and same transformation reuses the result, so unchanged data is not split or embedded again. Change a transformation setting (such as chunk size) and the hash changes, so affected work is redone. <code>pipeline.persist()</code> saves the cache to disk.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0087","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-018-4-2-ingestion-pipeline","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s017","q":"How does an ingestion pipeline with a docstore handle documents that were already ingested or have changed?","a":"<p>The docstore records <code>doc_id</code> to a hash of the content. Same id and same hash: skipped. Same id and different hash: reprocessed, and with a vector store attached the old nodes are replaced (an upsert). Without a vector store it can only detect and drop duplicate inputs. This is why documents need stable ids.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0088","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-018-4-2-ingestion-pipeline","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s017","q":"Why is <code>filename_as_id=True</code> useful when loading documents with <code>SimpleDirectoryReader</code> for an ingestion pipeline?","a":"<p>It makes the document id the file name, so ids are the same on every run. The docstore can then tell that a document is the same one and compare hashes. Random ids make every run look new, so you get duplicates in the vector store.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0089","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-018-4-2-ingestion-pipeline","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s017","q":"In an ingestion pipeline, why is the embedding model listed as one of the transformations?","a":"<p>Embedding is just another step that takes nodes and adds embeddings to them. Placing it last means nodes are already split, and then the pipeline can write finished nodes to the vector store. If the embed step is missing, nodes reach the store without vectors.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>pipeline = IngestionPipeline(\n    transformations=[\n        TokenTextSplitter(chunk_size=256, chunk_overlap=16),\n        Settings.embed_model,\n    ],\n    vector_store=vector_store,\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0090","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-018-4-2-ingestion-pipeline","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s017","q":"What is the order of transformations in an ingestion pipeline and why does it matter?","a":"<p>They run in the listed order, each taking the output nodes of the previous one. Splitting must come before embedding, and metadata extraction usually goes between them, so that metadata is computed per chunk and can be part of what is embedded. A wrong order can embed whole documents, or fail on size limits.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0091","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-018-4-2-ingestion-pipeline","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s017","q":"In a LlamaIndex ingestion pipeline, how do you persist and restore the cache and docstore?","a":"<p>Call <code>pipeline.persist(\"./pipeline_storage\")</code> to write the docstore and cache files, and load them on the next start (<code>pipeline.load(...)</code>) before <code>run</code>. Without that, the cache and the document hashes are lost between processes and everything is processed again.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0092","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-019-4-3-query-pipeline","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s018","q":"What is <code>QueryPipeline</code> in LlamaIndex 0.10.x and what does it let you build?","a":"<p>It is a declarative way to chain prompts, LLMs, retrievers, rerankers, query engines and other pipelines, either as a simple sequential chain or as a DAG with branches. It reduces glue code and gives a debug mode. In later LlamaIndex releases it is deprecated in favour of Workflows, so check your version.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0093","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-019-4-3-query-pipeline","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s018","q":"When do you use <code>QueryPipeline(chain=[...])</code> and when do you need <code>add_modules</code> and <code>add_link</code>?","a":"<p>Use <code>chain=</code> for a linear flow where each module takes one input and passes one output. Use <code>add_modules</code> and <code>add_link</code> when the same input must reach several modules, or a module needs several named inputs, for example a retriever's nodes and the question both going into a summarizer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0094","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-019-4-3-query-pipeline","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s018","q":"In a query pipeline's explicit-links form, why are <code>dest_key</code> values such as \"query_str\" and \"nodes\" needed?","a":"<p>The summarizer has two inputs, the question and the retrieved nodes, and <code>dest_key</code> says which named argument each link feeds. Without it LlamaIndex cannot tell which output goes where.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>p.add_link(&quot;input&quot;, &quot;retriever&quot;)\np.add_link(&quot;input&quot;, &quot;tree_summarizer&quot;, dest_key=&quot;query_str&quot;)\np.add_link(&quot;retriever&quot;, &quot;tree_summarizer&quot;, dest_key=&quot;nodes&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0095","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-019-4-3-query-pipeline","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s018","q":"How do you inspect what every step of a LlamaIndex query pipeline received and returned?","a":"<p>Create it with <code>verbose=True</code> for printed progress, or call <code>run_with_intermediates(...)</code>, which returns the final output plus a record of each module's inputs and outputs. This shows where a chain went wrong, for example that the retriever returned nothing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0096","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-019-4-3-query-pipeline","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s018","q":"What is the role of <code>InputComponent</code> in a LlamaIndex query pipeline?","a":"<p>It is a pass-through module that marks the pipeline's input so it can be linked to several downstream modules. You need it when one user input feeds, for instance, both a retriever and a summarizer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0097","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-022-4-5-prompt-engineering-for-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s020","q":"How do you see and change the prompts a LlamaIndex query engine uses?","a":"<p><code>query_engine.get_prompts()</code> returns a dict of prompt templates by key, and <code>query_engine.update_prompts({key: new_template})</code> replaces one. The key for the answer prompt is <code>response_synthesizer:text_qa_template</code>. Inspect the defaults first, then edit, so you keep the placeholders the engine fills.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0098","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-022-4-5-prompt-engineering-for-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s020","q":"In LlamaIndex, which placeholders must a custom <code>text_qa_template</code> contain, and what happens if you drop one?","a":"<p><code>{context_str}</code> receives the retrieved text and <code>{query_str}</code> the user question. If <code>{context_str}</code> is missing, the LLM never sees the retrieved chunks, so you lose the point of RAG. If <code>{query_str}</code> is missing, the LLM does not see the question.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>template = PromptTemplate(\n    &quot;Context:\\n{context_str}\\n\\nQuestion: {query_str}\\nAnswer:&quot;\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0099","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-022-4-5-prompt-engineering-for-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s020","q":"What should a good RAG answer prompt tell the LLM to do?","a":"<p>Answer only from the supplied context, say it does not know when the context lacks the answer, keep the requested format and tone, and optionally cite the passages used. Telling the model to ignore outside knowledge reduces hallucination, and an explicit refusal path stops it from inventing an answer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0100","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-022-4-5-prompt-engineering-for-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s020","q":"Why does changing the prompt not fix a retrieval problem?","a":"<p>The prompt only shapes how the LLM uses the context it receives. If the retrieved chunks are missing the answer, no wording can create it. Check retrieval first, then tune the prompt.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0101","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-023-4-6-response-synthesizers-response-modes","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s021","q":"In LlamaIndex, how do <code>refine</code> and <code>compact</code> response modes differ?","a":"<p><code>refine</code> starts with an answer from the first node, then makes one LLM call per remaining node to improve the answer, so it takes the most calls but handles each chunk separately. <code>compact</code> first packs as many chunks as fit into each prompt, then refines over those packed prompts, so it makes fewer calls. <code>compact</code> is the default for query engines in LlamaIndex 0.10+.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0102","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-023-4-6-response-synthesizers-response-modes","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s021","q":"What does <code>tree_summarize</code> do and when is it the best mode?","a":"<p>It summarises groups of chunks, then summarises those summaries, bottom-up, until one root answer remains. It suits questions that need a complete summary across many chunks or documents. It costs more calls than <code>simple_summarize</code> but handles inputs larger than the context window.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0103","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-023-4-6-response-synthesizers-response-modes","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s021","q":"What is the risk of <code>simple_summarize</code>?","a":"<p>It merges all retrieved chunks into one prompt with a single LLM call. That is fast and cheap, but if the merged text exceeds the context window it is truncated and information is lost. Use it only when top-k times chunk size is safely under the limit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0104","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-023-4-6-response-synthesizers-response-modes","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s021","q":"What is <code>no_text</code> response mode for, and what do you read afterwards?","a":"<p>It runs retrieval and postprocessing but makes no LLM call. You inspect <code>response.source_nodes</code> to see what would have been sent. It is a cheap way to debug retrieval.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0105","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-023-4-6-response-synthesizers-response-modes","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s021","q":"How does <code>accumulate</code> differ from <code>compact</code> or <code>refine</code>?","a":"<p>It runs the same query against each chunk separately and concatenates the individual answers, rather than combining chunks into one answer. Use it when you want one answer per chunk, for example per document. <code>compact_accumulate</code> packs chunks first to save calls.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0106","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-023-4-6-response-synthesizers-response-modes","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s021","q":"How do you choose a response mode for a LlamaIndex query engine?","a":"<p>Look at the question and the data: <code>compact</code> as a default balance of cost and quality, <code>refine</code> when each chunk must be examined and cost is secondary, <code>tree_summarize</code> for broad summaries, <code>simple_summarize</code> for small contexts and speed, and <code>no_text</code> for debugging. Test on your own evaluation questions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0107","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-023-4-6-response-synthesizers-response-modes","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s021","q":"How do you set the response mode of an engine?","a":"<p>Create the synthesizer with the chosen mode and pass it to <code>as_query_engine</code>. You can also pass <code>response_mode=\"tree_summarize\"</code> directly to <code>as_query_engine</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from llama_index.core import get_response_synthesizer\n\nsynthesizer = get_response_synthesizer(response_mode=&quot;tree_summarize&quot;)\nquery_engine = index.as_query_engine(response_synthesizer=synthesizer)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0108","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-024-4-7-data-preparation-for-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s022","q":"Why is data preparation often the biggest lever for RAG quality?","a":"<p>The system retrieves only what the index holds. Noisy text (headers, footers, broken words), lost tables, and missing metadata give poor embeddings and wrong chunks, and neither a better LLM nor a better prompt can repair that. Garbage in, garbage out applies directly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0109","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-024-4-7-data-preparation-for-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s022","q":"What problems make real PDFs hard to use for RAG?","a":"<p>Inconsistent layouts and columns, headers and footers repeated on every page, page numbers inside the text, tables and charts that plain text extraction flattens, scanned pages that need OCR, hyphenated line breaks, and footnotes mixed into body text.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0110","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-024-4-7-data-preparation-for-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s022","q":"What three levels of information should you consider when preparing a document?","a":"<p>Content (the text itself), elements (title, narrative text, list items, tables, images, each handled differently), and metadata (file name, type, page number, section) used for filtering, citations and hybrid search.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0111","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-024-4-7-data-preparation-for-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s022","q":"In LlamaIndex, how do <code>SimpleDirectoryReader</code> and a layout-aware loader such as <code>SmartPDFLoader</code> differ on a PDF?","a":"<p><code>SimpleDirectoryReader</code> (and <code>PDFReader</code>) returns one document per page with the plain extracted text. A layout-aware loader (LLMSherpa-based <code>SmartPDFLoader</code>) parses structure such as sections and tables, so it returns more, smaller, structure-aware chunks. Layout-aware parsing keeps tables and headings intact but needs an extra parsing service or API.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0112","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-024-4-7-data-preparation-for-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s022","q":"Which tools would you consider for tables or scanned documents instead of plain text extraction?","a":"<p>LlamaParse, pdfplumber or Camelot for tables, Unstructured for mixed layouts, OCR tools or a layout model for scans, and marker to convert PDFs to markdown. Pick on your document type, cost, and whether data may leave your network (hosted parsers send the file out).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0113","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-024-4-7-data-preparation-for-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s022","q":"When would you write your own extraction with PyMuPDF instead of using a ready loader?","a":"<p>When documents share a known layout and you need exact control: select page ranges, drop headers and footers, clean text, and attach your own metadata per page. It takes more code but gives predictable output. A generic loader is faster to start with and is enough for clean text.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0114","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-025-4-7-data-preparation-for-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s022","q":"In PyMuPDF, what does <code>page.get_text(\"text\", sort=True)</code> do, and why use <code>sort=True</code>?","a":"<p>It extracts the text of a page. <code>sort=True</code> orders text blocks by position (top to bottom, left to right) instead of internal file order, so the reading order is closer to what a human sees. Without it, text from different parts of a page can come out in a scrambled order.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0115","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-025-4-7-data-preparation-for-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s022","q":"Why strip headers and footers before chunking?","a":"<p>They repeat on every page, so they add the same words to many chunks, which lowers embedding quality, makes unrelated chunks look similar, and wastes tokens. Removing them also keeps page numbers and book titles out of the answer text. Doing it per document type is common because the layout differs by source.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0116","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-025-4-7-data-preparation-for-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s022","q":"Why persist cleaned documents once instead of cleaning on every run?","a":"<p>Cleaning a large PDF set is slow and deterministic. Saving the cleaned <code>Document</code> objects to a docstore (<code>storage_context.persist(...)</code>) lets later steps load them in seconds and guarantees every experiment starts from the same data. Reload with <code>SimpleDocumentStore.from_persist_dir</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0117","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-025-4-7-data-preparation-for-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s022","q":"What is the risk of cleaning options such as removing non-ASCII characters or lowercasing text?","a":"<p>They can destroy useful content: accented names, other languages, symbols, code and case-sensitive terms. Apply only the cleaning your data needs, and check samples before and after.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0118","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-025-4-7-data-preparation-for-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s022","q":"Which metadata would you attach to each page or chunk, and what do you use it for?","a":"<p>Source file name, title, author, page or section, and date. Uses: show citations, filter at query time (by author or date), support hybrid search, and trace a bad answer to its origin. Keep metadata small if it is also embedded or sent to the LLM, since it consumes tokens.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0119","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-026-4-7-data-preparation-for-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s022","q":"What does it mean to remove \"split words\" or hyphenation breaks when cleaning extracted text, and how can a regex over-fix?","a":"<p>PDFs break words across lines as \"impor- tant\", and the regex joins the pieces into \"important\". It also joins legitimate hyphenated compounds written with a space after the hyphen, such as \"well- known\" or a list item like \"pre- and post-\", so check samples. Fixing it improves embeddings because tokens are real words.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import re\n\ndef merge_hyphenated_words(text):\n    return re.sub(r&quot;(\\w+)-\\s+(\\w+)&quot;, r&quot;\\1\\2&quot;, text)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0120","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-026-4-7-data-preparation-for-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s022","q":"What does removing citation markers like <code>[12]</code> do for retrieval, and what is the downside?","a":"<p>It removes noise that adds meaningless tokens to chunks. The downside is that the reference link is lost if you need citations from the source text. For books or articles where numbers add nothing, removal is safe. For academic or legal text, keep them as metadata instead.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0121","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-026-4-7-data-preparation-for-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s022","q":"Does the order of cleaning steps matter? Give an example.","a":"<p>Yes. Collapse whitespace after removing other elements, otherwise gaps left by the removal stay. Merge split words after fixing line breaks. Lowercasing before a case-sensitive step (such as detecting headings) would break that step. Apply cleaning in a fixed, tested order.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0122","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-026-4-7-data-preparation-for-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s022","q":"Which tools would you use for academic PDFs with equations and references?","a":"<p>GROBID (structured XML output with references), nougat (a model that converts pages to markup including LaTeX), and LaTeX-OCR for formulas. Plain text extraction destroys equations.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0123","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-026-4-7-data-preparation-for-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s022","q":"How would you check the quality of your preparation before building the index?","a":"<p>Sample pages and read the cleaned text, compare against the original, count documents and characters per source, look for repeated header text and odd characters, and run a few test queries to see whether chunks make sense. A short manual review is cheaper than debugging bad retrieval later.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0124","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-027-4-8-putting-it-together-naive-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s023","q":"What does \"naive RAG\" mean, and what is its flow?","a":"<p>Naive RAG is the basic pipeline with default settings and no optimisation: load, split into chunks, embed, store, retrieve top-k by similarity, and generate with the LLM. It is the baseline to measure improvements against.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0125","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-027-4-8-putting-it-together-naive-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s023","q":"Why can large chunks of around 1000 tokens hurt the precision of a naive RAG system?","a":"<p>A large chunk blends several ideas, so its embedding is less specific and matches many queries loosely. The top-k results also use up much of the LLM context window with irrelevant text. Many applications get better results with smaller chunks, which you confirm with an evaluation set.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0126","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-027-4-8-putting-it-together-naive-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s023","q":"Why build a baseline naive RAG before trying advanced techniques?","a":"<p>It gives numbers to compare against. Without a baseline you cannot tell whether reranking, query rewriting or hybrid search helped, and complex techniques add cost and failure modes. Add one change at a time and measure it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0127","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-027-4-8-putting-it-together-naive-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s023","q":"Why can a naive RAG system answer a broad question such as \"what are the main themes across a whole corpus\" poorly?","a":"<p>Top-k similarity returns a few chunks that match the wording, but a broad question needs information spread across the whole corpus. Retrieval cannot see all of it, so the answer covers only the retrieved part. Fixes are summary indexes, hierarchical retrieval, tree summarisation and agentic approaches.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0128","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-027-4-8-putting-it-together-naive-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s023","q":"What does <code>llm</code> resolve to in <code>index.as_query_engine()</code> when you pass none?","a":"<p>It uses <code>Settings.llm</code>, the global default, and if that is not set LlamaIndex 0.10+ falls back to an OpenAI model, which needs an OpenAI key. Set <code>Settings.llm</code> explicitly to avoid surprises.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0129","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-028-4-9-drawbacks-of-naive-rag-interview-fav","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s024","q":"What are the main weaknesses of naive RAG at the indexing stage?","a":"<p>Poor extraction (lost tables, noise), chunks that split ideas or mix several topics, inefficient indexing of large data, and embeddings that capture meaning badly (jargon, numbers, different languages). All of these lower the quality of what can be retrieved.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0130","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-028-4-9-drawbacks-of-naive-rag-interview-fav","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s024","q":"What are the retrieval-stage problems of naive RAG?","a":"<p>Low precision (retrieved chunks do not match the need), low recall (relevant chunks are missed), poorly worded user queries that do not match the document wording, and redundant or overlapping chunks that waste context slots.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0131","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-028-4-9-drawbacks-of-naive-rag-interview-fav","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s024","q":"What are the generation-stage problems of naive RAG?","a":"<p>Hallucination not grounded in the context, difficulty judging whether the retrieved context is relevant, over-reliance on the context (copying it without synthesis), and output that is biased, inconsistent or irrelevant.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0132","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-028-4-9-drawbacks-of-naive-rag-interview-fav","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s024","q":"Define precision and recall for retrieval and say which one reranking improves.","a":"<p>Precision is the share of retrieved chunks that are relevant. Recall is the share of all relevant chunks that were retrieved. Retrieving more chunks raises recall and usually lowers precision. Reranking improves precision: fetch a wide set for recall, then reorder and keep the best.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0133","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-028-4-9-drawbacks-of-naive-rag-interview-fav","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s024","q":"Which techniques address which naive RAG weakness?","a":"<p>Better data preparation and chunking address indexing. Query rewriting, hybrid search, metadata filters and reranking address retrieval. Prompt compression, better prompts, and faithfulness checks address generation. Fix the stage that your evaluation shows is weakest.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0134","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-028-4-9-drawbacks-of-naive-rag-interview-fav","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s024","q":"A user's question uses different words than the document. Why does naive RAG fail, and what can you do?","a":"<p>Embedding search is semantic but imperfect, and short questions often sit far from long answer passages. Options: rewrite or expand the query, generate a hypothetical answer and embed it (HyDE), add hybrid keyword search, or index hypothetical questions for each chunk.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0135","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-029-5-rag-evaluation","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s025","q":"Why evaluate a RAG system, and what does it let you do?","a":"<p>Evaluation gives numbers for accuracy and relevance, so you can compare models, prompts, chunk sizes and retrieval strategies, track quality over time, catch regressions, and find which stage (retrieval or generation) to fix. Without it, changes are guesses.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0136","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-029-5-rag-evaluation","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s025","q":"Why do you evaluate retrieval and generation separately?","a":"<p>A wrong answer can come from either stage, and the fix differs. If the right context was not retrieved, change indexing or retrieval. If it was retrieved but the answer is wrong, change the prompt, the LLM or the synthesis mode. Combined scores hide which one failed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0137","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-029-5-rag-evaluation","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s025","q":"What is the hardest part of building RAG evaluation, and why?","a":"<p>A high-quality evaluation dataset of realistic questions with reference answers or contexts. Metrics are easy to compute once you have one. Hand-writing hundreds of questions is slow, so teams often use an LLM to draft them and then filter or review.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0138","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-029-5-rag-evaluation","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s025","q":"Why should RAG evaluation be repeated after each change?","a":"<p>Changing chunk size, embedding model, top-k or prompts can improve one question type and hurt another. A fixed evaluation set run after every change shows the net effect, and it works as a regression test.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0139","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-030-5-1-what-to-evaluate","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s026","q":"Which two components does RAG evaluation look at, and what does each need to prove?","a":"<p>Retrieval: filtering is effective and the retrieved data is relevant and useful. Generation: the LLM uses the context well and the answer is factual, relevant and coherent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0140","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-030-5-1-what-to-evaluate","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s026","q":"Name the four abilities used to test the robustness of a RAG system.","a":"<p>Noise robustness (answer well when context has irrelevant text), negative rejection (say \"I don't know\" when the context lacks the answer), information integration (combine facts from several chunks), and counterfactual robustness (ignore wrong information in the context).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0141","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-030-5-1-what-to-evaluate","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s026","q":"How would you test negative rejection?","a":"<p>Ask questions whose answers are not in your data and check that the system declines instead of inventing something. Score the share of such questions answered with an appropriate refusal. A prompt that allows \"I don't know\" and a similarity cutoff both help.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0142","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-030-5-1-what-to-evaluate","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s026","q":"How would you test noise robustness?","a":"<p>Add irrelevant or distracting chunks to the retrieved context (or lower the similarity cutoff so noisy chunks come in) and measure whether answer accuracy drops compared with clean context.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0143","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-031-5-2-metrics","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s027","q":"What do faithfulness, answer relevance and context relevance each measure?","a":"<p>Faithfulness: the answer stays true to the retrieved context. Answer relevance: the answer addresses the question and stays on topic. Context relevance: the retrieved context is targeted and has little irrelevant text. They split the pipeline into generation grounding, answer quality and retrieval quality.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0144","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-031-5-2-metrics","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s027","q":"What is MRR, and how is it computed?","a":"<p>Mean Reciprocal Rank. For each query take 1 divided by the rank of the first correct result (1 for first place, 0.5 for second, 0 if absent), then average over queries. It rewards putting the first relevant chunk near the top. It looks only at the first hit, so it ignores other relevant results.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0145","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-031-5-2-metrics","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s027","q":"What is hit rate in retrieval evaluation, and how does it differ from MRR?","a":"<p>Hit rate is the fraction of queries where the correct chunk appears anywhere in the top-k results. MRR also uses the rank: it averages 1/rank of the first correct chunk. Hit rate says whether retrieval found the chunk, and MRR says how high it was placed. Two systems can have the same hit rate but different MRR.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0146","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-031-5-2-metrics","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s027","q":"Compute MRR for three queries whose first correct chunks are at ranks 1, 2 and 4.","a":"<p>(1 + 1/2 + 1/4) / 3 = 1.75 / 3, which is about 0.583.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0147","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-031-5-2-metrics","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s027","q":"What is LLM-as-a-judge, and why is it used for RAG metrics?","a":"<p>A strong LLM scores another system's output against criteria (for example \"is this answer supported by the context?\"). It is cheaper, faster and more scalable than human review and agrees reasonably with human ratings, which allows frequent evaluation runs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0148","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-031-5-2-metrics","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s027","q":"What are the known weaknesses of LLM-as-a-judge?","a":"<p>Position bias (favouring the first or second answer), verbosity bias (favouring longer answers), self-preference, weak judgement on topics like math or fine factual detail, and sensitivity to the judge prompt. Mitigate by using a strong judge, a clear rubric, swapping answer order, and checking a sample against human labels.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0149","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-031-5-2-metrics","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s027","q":"When would you prefer retrieval metrics such as hit rate or MRR over LLM-judged metrics?","a":"<p>When you have labelled relevant chunks per query. They are cheap, deterministic and need no LLM calls, and they isolate retrieval from generation. LLM-judged metrics are for cases without labels or for qualities such as faithfulness that rank metrics cannot see.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0150","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-032-5-3-building-an-evaluation-set-synthetic","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s028","q":"How do you build a synthetic evaluation set for RAG from your own documents?","a":"<p>Sample documents, split them into chunks, have an LLM write a question answerable from each chunk, have an LLM answer it from that chunk, have a critic LLM score how answerable the question is from the chunk, filter low scores, then keep the question, context and answer as the evaluation set.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0151","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-032-5-3-building-an-evaluation-set-synthetic","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s028","q":"Why sample a balanced number of documents per author or source when building the evaluation set?","a":"<p>Otherwise large documents or authors dominate and the set measures only them. Balanced sampling checks that the system works across all your content, and it makes per-source results comparable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0152","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-032-5-3-building-an-evaluation-set-synthetic","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s028","q":"Why use a stronger model as the critic than the one that wrote the questions?","a":"<p>Judging answerability needs reliable reasoning, and a weak critic misses bad questions. Using a different and stronger model also avoids a model approving its own mistakes (self-preference). Questions are cheap to write, so spend the quality budget on the filter.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0153","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-032-5-3-building-an-evaluation-set-synthetic","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s028","q":"What does a groundedness filter do, and why drop low-scoring questions?","a":"<p>The critic rates how clearly the question can be answered from its source chunk (for example 1 to 5). Questions scoring low are vague or depend on missing context, so a correct RAG system would still fail them and the metrics would be misleading. Keeping scores of 4 or more gives a cleaner set.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0154","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-032-5-3-building-an-evaluation-set-synthetic","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s028","q":"Why should synthetic questions be written to look like real user queries?","a":"<p>Questions copied from chunk wording make retrieval too easy because they share words with the answer text. Real users write short, informal or vague queries. Prompting for the style of a search query, in the first person and without qualifiers, tests a harder and more realistic case.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0155","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-032-5-3-building-an-evaluation-set-synthetic","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s028","q":"Why does the LLM that writes the reference answers need to be told to answer only from the context?","a":"<p>Otherwise it mixes in its own knowledge, so the reference may not match what is in your documents. A reference answer that is not grounded in the chunk punishes a RAG system for being faithful.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0156","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-032-5-3-building-an-evaluation-set-synthetic","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s028","q":"What are the limits of synthetic evaluation sets?","a":"<p>They reflect the generating LLM's style, may leak wording from the chunks, cover only content that was sampled, and cannot replace real user questions. Use them to start, then add real queries from logs and review a sample by hand.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0157","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-033-5-3-building-an-evaluation-set-synthetic","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s028","q":"Why build a RAG evaluation set synthetically from your own documents instead of relying on a public benchmark?","a":"<p>A public benchmark tests general knowledge and different documents, so it says little about how your retriever and prompts behave on your data. Synthetic questions are created from your own chunks, so each question has a known context and a reference answer. The trade-off is quality: LLM-written questions can be trivial, ambiguous or unanswerable, so you need a filtering step and ideally a human spot check.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0158","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-033-5-3-building-an-evaluation-set-synthetic","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s028","q":"When an LLM writes one evaluation question per chunk, why do you instruct it to write short, search-style questions that a specific piece of the chunk can answer?","a":"<p>Real users type short queries, so test questions should look like them. Requiring that a concise piece of the context answers the question gives each question a clear ground-truth passage, which you need to measure retrieval (did the right chunk come back?). Long, multi-clause questions copy wording from the chunk, which makes retrieval look better than it will be in production.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0159","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-033-5-3-building-an-evaluation-set-synthetic","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s028","q":"In LlamaIndex, what do <code>PromptTemplate(...).format(context_str=...)</code> and <code>llm.complete(prompt)</code> each do when you create evaluation data in a loop?","a":"<p><code>PromptTemplate.format</code> fills the <code>{context_str}</code> placeholder and returns a plain prompt string. <code>llm.complete</code> sends that string to the LLM as a single completion call (no chat history) and returns a <code>CompletionResponse</code>, whose <code>.text</code> is the model output. Using a template keeps the instructions fixed while only the chunk changes between calls.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from llama_index.core.prompts.base import PromptTemplate\n\ntemplate = PromptTemplate(&quot;Write a question about: {context_str}&quot;)\nprompt = template.format(context_str=chunk_text)\nresponse = llm.complete(prompt)\nquestion = response.text</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0160","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-033-5-3-building-an-evaluation-set-synthetic","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s028","q":"Why is it better to write one question per chunk than one per whole document when you want to test retrieval?","a":"<p>Retrieval returns chunks, so a question tied to one chunk gives a clear target: the right chunk should appear in the top-k. A question about a whole document can be answered by several chunks, so you cannot tell whether a miss is a retrieval failure or just a different valid chunk. The weakness is that single-chunk questions do not test multi-hop or cross-document questions, so add those separately.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0161","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-033-5-3-building-an-evaluation-set-synthetic","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s028","q":"What is a common failure of LLM-written evaluation questions, and how do you catch it?","a":"<p>Common failures are questions that depend on context the user would not have (\"according to the passage...\"), questions that are too vague to have one right answer, and questions that cannot be answered from the chunk. You catch them by scoring each question for answerability with a critic LLM, filtering low scores, and manually reading a random sample.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0162","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-033-5-3-building-an-evaluation-set-synthetic","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s028","q":"Why is it risky to use the same LLM to write the questions, write the reference answers and later judge the RAG answers?","a":"<p>Models tend to prefer their own style and can share the same blind spots, so scores are biased upward and errors go unnoticed. Use a stronger or different model as the judge where you can, keep the judge prompt separate, and validate the judge against a small set of human labels.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0163","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-034-5-3-building-an-evaluation-set-synthetic","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s028","q":"What is fragile about parsing the critic's score this way, and how would you make it safer?","a":"<p>It depends on the LLM repeating the exact text \"Total rating: \" and ending the reply right after the number. Any extra text raises a <code>ValueError</code>, which the bare <code>except</code> hides by storing <code>None</code>, so many questions can silently end up unscored. Safer options: ask for JSON or use structured output with a Pydantic model, validate the range 1-5, and count the <code>None</code> results so failures are visible.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>response_string = response.text\ntry:\n    score_as_int = int(response_string.split(&quot;Total rating: &quot;)[-1].strip())\n    question[&#x27;question_groundedness_score&#x27;] = score_as_int\nexcept Exception as e:\n    question[&#x27;question_groundedness_score&#x27;] = None</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0164","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-034-5-3-building-an-evaluation-set-synthetic","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s028","q":"After scoring, how do you decide the filter threshold for an evaluation set?","a":"<p>Look at the distribution of scores and read a sample at each level. A strict cutoff (score 4 or 5) gives cleaner questions but fewer of them, a loose one gives more questions with some noise. Pick the strictest cutoff that still leaves enough questions per topic to make your metrics statistically meaningful.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0165","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-034-5-3-building-an-evaluation-set-synthetic","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s028","q":"Why keep the source context and the reference answer next to each question in the stored evaluation set?","a":"<p>Retrieval metrics need the ground-truth context (did you retrieve it?), and answer metrics such as correctness need the reference answer. Storing question, context, answer and the critic score together also lets you re-filter later without regenerating the data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0166","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-034-5-3-building-an-evaluation-set-synthetic","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s028","q":"How do you share and reuse a synthetic evaluation set with the Hugging Face <code>datasets</code> library?","a":"<p><code>Dataset.from_list</code> turns a list of dicts into a dataset, <code>push_to_hub</code> uploads it to a repository on the Hugging Face Hub (this needs authentication), and <code>load_dataset</code> downloads it by repository id. Publishing means the expensive LLM generation is done once and every experiment, or teammate, starts from the same fixed set, which makes runs comparable.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from datasets import Dataset, load_dataset\n\nrag_eval_set = Dataset.from_list(records)\nrag_eval_set.push_to_hub(&quot;my-org/my-rag-eval-set&quot;)\nreloaded = load_dataset(&quot;my-org/my-rag-eval-set&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0167","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-034-5-3-building-an-evaluation-set-synthetic","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s028","q":"When you run a RAG pipeline over every question of an evaluation set on an API with strict rate limits, how do you avoid failures?","a":"<p>Add throttling: sleep between batches of calls, limit concurrency, and retry with exponential backoff on rate-limit errors. Also cache results, so a crash does not force a rerun of all questions. Paid tiers or a local model remove the limit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0168","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-034-5-3-building-an-evaluation-set-synthetic","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s028","q":"Why store the pipeline's answers as a new column of the evaluation dataset (for example with <code>Dataset.add_column</code>)?","a":"<p>Each experiment (chunk size, reranker, prompt) produces its own answer column next to the same questions and reference answers. You can then score every column with the same evaluators and compare experiments row by row, without rerunning the other configurations.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0169","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-035-6-1-three-rag-paradigms-from-the-rag-for","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s029","q":"What are the three RAG paradigms in the \"RAG for Large Language Models: A Survey\" paper, and how do they differ?","a":"<p>Naive RAG indexes (clean, chunk, embed), retrieves by similarity and generates with the query plus documents in one prompt. Advanced RAG keeps that flow but adds optimization before retrieval (better indexing, metadata, query rewriting) and after it (reranking, prompt compression). Modular RAG breaks the system into replaceable modules (search, memory, routing, and so on) that can be rearranged, so the pipeline need not be a fixed line.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0170","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-035-6-1-three-rag-paradigms-from-the-rag-for","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s029","q":"In advanced RAG, which techniques are pre-retrieval and which are post-retrieval?","a":"<p>Pre-retrieval work improves what is indexed and what is searched: chunking and granularity, metadata, index structure, query rewriting or expansion, hypothetical questions or documents. Post-retrieval work improves the retrieved set: reranking, filtering, reordering and prompt compression. A rule of thumb is that pre-retrieval raises recall and post-retrieval raises precision and prompt quality.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0171","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-035-6-1-three-rag-paradigms-from-the-rag-for","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s029","q":"Why does metadata on chunks help retrieval quality?","a":"<p>Chunks with similar wording look alike in embedding space, and metadata (author, date, section, source type) lets you filter or disambiguate them before or during the vector search. It also enables structured queries such as \"only documents after 2023\". Its quality depends on correct tagging.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0172","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-035-6-1-three-rag-paradigms-from-the-rag-for","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s029","q":"How does Modular RAG differ from simply adding more steps to an advanced RAG pipeline?","a":"<p>Advanced RAG still follows a fixed retrieve-then-generate order with extra steps. Modular RAG treats search, routing, memory, fusion and so on as modules with defined interfaces, so you can branch, loop, route per query or swap a module. The cost is more design and evaluation work.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0173","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-036-6-1-three-rag-paradigms-from-the-rag-for","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s029","q":"What are \"hypothetical questions\" for index alignment in advanced RAG?","a":"<p>At indexing time an LLM writes questions each chunk can answer, and these questions are stored or embedded with the chunk. At query time the user question is matched to similar questions, which is usually closer in form than question versus passage. The cost is extra LLM calls during ingestion.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0174","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-036-6-1-three-rag-paradigms-from-the-rag-for","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s029","q":"What are step-back prompting, sub-queries and HyDE, and what do they share?","a":"<p>Step-back prompting asks a broader question first to retrieve background. Sub-queries split a complex question into parts that are retrieved separately. HyDE has the LLM write a hypothetical answer and searches with its embedding. All three rewrite the query before retrieval, so the retriever sees a better search input than the raw user wording.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0175","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-036-6-1-three-rag-paradigms-from-the-rag-for","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s029","q":"What can dynamic or fine-tuned embeddings add to a RAG system compared with a general-purpose embedding model?","a":"<p>A general model may not capture domain vocabulary or what \"relevant\" means in your domain. Fine-tuning on domain pairs improves similarity for those terms, and dynamic embeddings adapt to context. The costs are labelled data, training and re-embedding the whole corpus whenever the model changes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0176","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-036-6-1-three-rag-paradigms-from-the-rag-for","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s029","q":"In what order should you try RAG optimizations, if you have limited time?","a":"<p>Start with the cheap, high-impact ones: fix chunking and chunk size, add metadata and hybrid search, then add reranking. Next try query transformations, then heavier options such as summary indexes, agentic routing or fine-tuned embeddings. Measure each change on a fixed evaluation set so you keep only what helps.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0177","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-037-6-2-optimizing-chunk-size","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s030","q":"How does chunk size affect a RAG system?","a":"<p>Small chunks give precise embeddings and cheap prompts but may lack the context needed to answer. Large chunks carry more context but dilute the embedding with unrelated text, cost more tokens and can bury the answer. Chunk size therefore affects relevance, faithfulness and response time, and the best value depends on the documents and the questions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0178","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-037-6-2-optimizing-chunk-size","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s030","q":"In LlamaIndex 0.10.x, what are the default chunk size and overlap, and what does overlap do?","a":"<p>The default node parser used through <code>Settings</code> has a chunk size of 1024 tokens and an overlap of 20. Constructing <code>SentenceSplitter()</code> directly uses 1024 and an overlap of 200, so the two defaults differ; set both explicitly. Overlap repeats tokens at the boundary of neighboring chunks, so a sentence cut at a boundary still appears whole in at least one chunk. More overlap protects context but increases index size and duplicate results.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0179","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-037-6-2-optimizing-chunk-size","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s030","q":"If you cut the chunk size in half, what else should you adjust in retrieval?","a":"<p>Raise <code>similarity_top_k</code>. Smaller chunks hold less information each, so to give the LLM about the same amount of context you need to retrieve more of them, for example 512 tokens with a higher top-k instead of 1024 tokens with a lower one. Also watch the prompt budget and the added noise.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0180","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-037-6-2-optimizing-chunk-size","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s030","q":"How do <code>TokenTextSplitter</code> and <code>SentenceSplitter</code> differ in LlamaIndex, and how do you choose?","a":"<p><code>TokenTextSplitter</code> cuts at an exact token count, so chunks are uniform but may end mid-sentence. <code>SentenceSplitter</code> respects sentence and paragraph boundaries, so chunks vary in size but stay readable and semantically coherent. Use <code>SentenceSplitter</code> for most prose, and <code>TokenTextSplitter</code> when you need a strict token limit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0181","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-037-6-2-optimizing-chunk-size","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s030","q":"How do you measure the best chunk size for your own documents?","a":"<p>Build an evaluation set, index the same documents with several chunk sizes (for example 128, 256, 512, 1024), and run the same questions on each. Compare retrieval metrics (hit rate, MRR), answer faithfulness and relevancy, latency and cost. Choose the smallest chunk size that maintains answer quality.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0182","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-037-6-2-optimizing-chunk-size","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s030","q":"In LlamaIndex, what does this snippet check, and what do you expect when <code>chunk_size</code> gets smaller?","a":"<p>It splits the documents into nodes of at most about 128 tokens with 16 tokens of overlap and prints the number of nodes. A smaller <code>chunk_size</code> gives more nodes, so the index is larger and embedding costs go up.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from llama_index.core.node_parser import SentenceSplitter\n\nsplitter = SentenceSplitter(chunk_size=128, chunk_overlap=16)\nnodes = splitter.get_nodes_from_documents(documents)\nprint(len(nodes))</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0183","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-038-6-2-optimizing-chunk-size","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s030","q":"What is Maximal Marginal Relevance (MMR) in retrieval, and what problem does it solve?","a":"<p>MMR re-selects results iteratively, picking the item that is relevant to the query but least similar to those already selected. It removes near-duplicate chunks, which are common when neighbors overlap, so the LLM sees more diverse information in the same top-k.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0184","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-038-6-2-optimizing-chunk-size","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s030","q":"In the MMR formula, what does the lambda parameter control?","a":"<p>Lambda trades relevance against diversity. Near 1 it behaves like plain similarity search. Near 0 it favors items that differ from those already picked. In LlamaIndex, <code>mmr_threshold</code> passed in <code>vector_store_kwargs</code> plays that role, enabled with <code>vector_store_query_mode=\"mmr\"</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># MMR = argmax [ lam * relevance(item, query) - (1 - lam) * max_similarity(item, selected) ]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0185","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-038-6-2-optimizing-chunk-size","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s030","q":"How do you turn on MMR for a LlamaIndex vector index query engine, and what must the vector store support?","a":"<p>Pass <code>vector_store_query_mode=\"mmr\"</code> and set <code>mmr_threshold</code> in <code>vector_store_kwargs</code>. The vector store integration must implement MMR; stores that do not will ignore or reject the mode. The result is the top-k chunks chosen for relevance plus diversity.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>query_engine = index.as_query_engine(\n    similarity_top_k=5,\n    vector_store_query_mode=&quot;mmr&quot;,\n    vector_store_kwargs={&quot;mmr_threshold&quot;: 0.4},\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0186","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-038-6-2-optimizing-chunk-size","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s030","q":"What is the default <code>similarity_top_k</code> in a LlamaIndex vector retriever, and what happens if you pass a misspelled keyword such as <code>similiarty_top_k</code> to <code>as_query_engine</code>?","a":"<p>The default is 2. Extra keyword arguments to <code>as_query_engine</code> are forwarded to the retriever and query-engine constructors, so a misspelling may not raise an error and the default may still apply, which silently changes your results. After configuring, verify with <code>len(response.source_nodes)</code> that you got the expected number of chunks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0187","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-039-6-3-small-to-big-retrieval-recursive-ret","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s031","q":"What is small-to-big retrieval and what problem does it solve?","a":"<p>Small chunks embed precisely, so they match a query well, but they lack context for the LLM. Small-to-big retrieves by small units and then hands the LLM larger surrounding or parent content. This gives retrieval precision and answer context together.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0188","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-039-6-3-small-to-big-retrieval-recursive-ret","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s031","q":"In LlamaIndex, how does <code>SentenceWindowNodeParser</code> work, and what does <code>window_size</code> do?","a":"<p>It creates one node per sentence and stores the surrounding sentences (<code>window_size</code> sentences on each side) in the node's metadata, with the sentence itself under <code>original_text</code>. Only the single sentence is embedded, because the window metadata is excluded from embedding and LLM text by default. At query time a postprocessor swaps the sentence for its window.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0189","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-039-6-3-small-to-big-retrieval-recursive-ret","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s031","q":"In a LlamaIndex sentence-window setup, why is <code>MetadataReplacementPostProcessor</code> required?","a":"<p>Without it the LLM would only see the single retrieved sentence, because that is the node text. The postprocessor replaces each node's text with the value under <code>target_metadata_key</code> (the window), so the LLM gets the surrounding context. It runs after retrieval, before synthesis, as a <code>node_postprocessors</code> entry of the query engine.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0190","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-039-6-3-small-to-big-retrieval-recursive-ret","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s031","q":"In LlamaIndex, what must be consistent between <code>SentenceWindowNodeParser</code> and <code>MetadataReplacementPostProcessor</code>?","a":"<p>The metadata key. The parser writes the window under <code>window_metadata_key</code> (default <code>\"window\"</code>), and the postprocessor reads <code>target_metadata_key</code>. If the names differ, nothing is replaced and the LLM keeps the single sentence, with no error.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>parser = SentenceWindowNodeParser(window_size=3, window_metadata_key=&quot;window&quot;)\npost = MetadataReplacementPostProcessor(target_metadata_key=&quot;window&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0191","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-039-6-3-small-to-big-retrieval-recursive-ret","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s031","q":"When splitting is already done by the node parser, what do you pass to LlamaIndex ingestion as transformations, and why not a splitter again?","a":"<p>Only the embedding model. The nodes are already sentence nodes with window metadata, so another splitter would re-chunk them and could break the metadata. Ingestion then just embeds the nodes and writes them to the vector store.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0192","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-039-6-3-small-to-big-retrieval-recursive-ret","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s031","q":"How do you see what the embedding model or the LLM receives for a LlamaIndex node?","a":"<p><code>get_content(metadata_mode=...)</code> controls which metadata is included: <code>\"all\"</code> shows everything stored, <code>\"llm\"</code> shows what the LLM gets, and <code>\"embed\"</code> what the embedding model gets. Keys listed in <code>excluded_llm_metadata_keys</code> or <code>excluded_embed_metadata_keys</code> are removed from the matching view. This is how you verify that a window is not embedded.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>print(node.get_content(metadata_mode=&quot;all&quot;))\nprint(node.get_content(metadata_mode=&quot;llm&quot;))</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0193","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-040-6-3-small-to-big-retrieval-recursive-ret","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s031","q":"How does the parent-child (small-to-big) approach in LlamaIndex work, and what role does <code>IndexNode</code> play?","a":"<p>You split each document into several small child chunks and create <code>IndexNode</code>s that point to the parent node's id with <code>IndexNode.from_text_node(child, parent_id)</code>. Retrieval matches a small child, and the <code>RecursiveRetriever</code> follows its reference to the parent, which is what goes to the LLM. An <code>IndexNode</code> is a node with a link to another object (node, retriever or query engine).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0194","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-040-6-3-small-to-big-retrieval-recursive-ret","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s031","q":"In LlamaIndex, what does <code>RecursiveRetriever</code> take as arguments, and how does it differ from <code>index.as_retriever()</code>?","a":"<p>It takes the id of the root retriever, a dict mapping ids to retrievers (<code>retriever_dict</code>) and a dict mapping node ids to nodes (<code>node_dict</code>) used to resolve references. It is a standalone class built from these parts, not a method of an index. It retrieves with the root, follows each <code>IndexNode</code> reference, deduplicates the resulting nodes and returns them.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>retriever = RecursiveRetriever(\n    &quot;vector&quot;,\n    retriever_dict={&quot;vector&quot;: base_retriever},\n    node_dict=all_nodes_dict,\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0195","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-040-6-3-small-to-big-retrieval-recursive-ret","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s031","q":"Why is it useful to index several child chunk sizes (such as 128, 256 and 512 tokens) for the same parent?","a":"<p>Different questions match different granularities: a specific fact matches a small chunk, a broader question matches a larger one. All children point to the same parent, so any match leads to the same full context. The cost is a larger index and more embedding calls.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0196","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-040-6-3-small-to-big-retrieval-recursive-ret","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s031","q":"In LlamaIndex, which class wraps a retriever such as <code>RecursiveRetriever</code> into a full question-answering engine?","a":"<p><code>RetrieverQueryEngine</code>, for example <code>RetrieverQueryEngine.from_args(retriever, llm=llm)</code>. A retriever only returns nodes. The query engine adds node postprocessing and response synthesis on top and returns an answer with its source nodes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0197","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-040-6-3-small-to-big-retrieval-recursive-ret","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s031","q":"How do you choose between the sentence-window approach and parent-child chunks for small-to-big retrieval?","a":"<p>Sentence window is simple and gives sentence-level precision with a fixed neighbourhood of context. Parent-child suits structured or hierarchical documents (sections, pages), where a match should expand to a meaningful unit and where you want several granularities. The parent-child design is more work to build and index.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0198","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-041-6-4-semantic-chunking","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s032","q":"What is semantic chunking and how does it differ from fixed-size chunking?","a":"<p>Semantic chunking uses embeddings to find the places where the meaning shifts and cuts there, so each chunk covers one topic. Fixed-size chunking cuts at a set number of tokens or sentences regardless of meaning. Semantic chunks keep related sentences together and vary in size, but ingestion is slower and costlier because sentences must be embedded.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0199","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-041-6-4-semantic-chunking","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s032","q":"In LlamaIndex, what steps does <code>SemanticSplitterNodeParser</code> take to decide where to split?","a":"<p>It splits the text into sentences, groups neighboring sentences by <code>buffer_size</code>, embeds each group, and computes the cosine distance (1 - similarity) between adjacent groups. Where a distance exceeds the chosen percentile (<code>breakpoint_percentile_threshold</code>) it starts a new chunk. The sentences between breakpoints form one node.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0200","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-041-6-4-semantic-chunking","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s032","q":"What does <code>buffer_size</code> do in LlamaIndex's <code>SemanticSplitterNodeParser</code>, and what is its default?","a":"<p>It sets how many sentences are combined when comparing neighbors. The default is 1, so each sentence is compared on its own. A larger buffer gives each comparison more context, which smooths noise from short sentences, but it can blur real topic changes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0201","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-041-6-4-semantic-chunking","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s032","q":"In LlamaIndex, what does <code>breakpoint_percentile_threshold</code> control, and what happens if you lower it?","a":"<p>It is the percentile of the distances between adjacent sentence groups beyond which a break is placed (default 95). Lowering it makes more positions count as breaks, so you get more and smaller chunks. Raising it gives fewer, larger chunks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0202","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-041-6-4-semantic-chunking","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s032","q":"What are the drawbacks of semantic chunking, and when would you not use it?","a":"<p>It needs an embedding call for every sentence group during ingestion, so it is slower and more expensive than fixed splitting, and chunk sizes are unpredictable (some can be very long). For uniform, short or well-structured documents, or when speed matters, a sentence splitter is enough. Use semantic chunking when documents move between topics without clear structure and retrieval accuracy matters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0203","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-041-6-4-semantic-chunking","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s032","q":"Which settings would you tune for semantic chunking when retrieval quality is poor?","a":"<p>The embedding model (and its dimensions), <code>buffer_size</code>, the breakpoint threshold, and the sentence splitter that runs first. Evaluate each variant on a fixed question set, and check the resulting chunk length distribution so that no chunk exceeds the embedding model's input limit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0204","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-042-6-5-metadata-extraction","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s033","q":"Why extract metadata for chunks in a RAG pipeline?","a":"<p>Similar-looking chunks become distinguishable, which improves retrieval, and structured fields enable filtering (by author, date, topic). Extracted summaries, titles, keywords and questions also add searchable text that can match user queries better than the raw chunk. The gain depends on tag quality, and extraction costs extra LLM calls at ingestion.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0205","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-042-6-5-metadata-extraction","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s033","q":"In LlamaIndex 0.10+, which metadata extractors are available, and what does each add?","a":"<p><code>SummaryExtractor</code> adds a summary of the node (and optionally of the previous or next node), <code>QuestionsAnsweredExtractor</code> adds questions the node can answer, <code>TitleExtractor</code> adds a title based on the surrounding nodes, and <code>KeywordExtractor</code> adds keywords. They are in <code>llama_index.core.extractors</code> and all use an LLM.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0206","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-042-6-5-metadata-extraction","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s033","q":"Where in an ingestion pipeline should metadata extractors be placed, and why?","a":"<p>After the splitter and before the embedding model. They need the chunked nodes to describe, and the embedding step must come last so the extracted metadata is already on the node when it is embedded. If an extractor ran after the embedding, its output would not influence the vectors.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>transforms = [text_splitter, qa_extractor, summary_extractor, embed_model]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0207","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-042-6-5-metadata-extraction","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s033","q":"What does <code>metadata_mode=MetadataMode.EMBED</code> in an extractor such as <code>QuestionsAnsweredExtractor</code> do?","a":"<p>It decides which metadata is visible when the extractor builds node text for the LLM or embedding: the <code>EMBED</code> mode includes metadata that is not in <code>excluded_embed_metadata_keys</code>. This matters because metadata included in embedding text changes the vector, so it can improve or distort matching.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0208","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-042-6-5-metadata-extraction","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s033","q":"In LlamaIndex, what does <code>SummaryExtractor(summaries=[\"prev\", \"self\", \"next\"])</code> produce?","a":"<p>For each node it writes a summary of the previous node, of the node itself and of the next node into the metadata. The neighbor summaries restore some context lost by chunking. The trade-off is three times the summary calls and longer node text.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0209","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-042-6-5-metadata-extraction","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s033","q":"How would you stop extracted metadata from changing a node's embedding while keeping it for filtering?","a":"<p>Add the key to the node's <code>excluded_embed_metadata_keys</code> (and <code>excluded_llm_metadata_keys</code> if the LLM should not see it either). The key stays in the metadata and can be used in metadata filters, but it is left out of the text sent to the embedding model.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0210","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-042-6-5-metadata-extraction","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s033","q":"What are the cost and quality trade-offs when you pick the LLM for metadata extraction?","a":"<p>A stronger LLM produces better titles, questions and summaries, but ingestion cost and time multiply because every chunk is processed. A cheaper model is often enough for keywords. Test on a sample, check retrieval metrics, and extract only what you will use.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0211","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-042-6-5-metadata-extraction","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s033","q":"When is it better to add metadata manually than to extract it with an LLM?","a":"<p>When you already know it reliably (author, date, document type, department), because a manual or rule-based value is exact, free and deterministic. LLM extraction is for fields you cannot derive from existing structure, such as topics or questions, and its output can be wrong.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0212","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-043-6-6-document-summary-index","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s034","q":"What is a Document Summary Index in LlamaIndex and what problem does it solve?","a":"<p>It stores an LLM summary for each document, plus the document's chunks and a mapping between them. Retrieval first finds relevant documents by their summaries, then returns the linked chunks. It addresses the lack of global context in single chunks, where the chunk text alone might not show what the document is about.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0213","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-043-6-6-document-summary-index","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s034","q":"What are the two retrieval modes of <code>DocumentSummaryIndex</code> in LlamaIndex, and how do you choose?","a":"<p><code>DocumentSummaryIndexLLMRetriever</code> has the LLM score batches of summaries (<code>choice_batch_size</code>, <code>choice_top_k</code>), which gives better judgement but is slower and costs more. <code>DocumentSummaryIndexEmbeddingRetriever</code> compares query and summary embeddings (<code>similarity_top_k</code>), which is fast and cheap but depends on embedding quality. Use embeddings by default and the LLM retriever when precision matters more than cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0214","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-043-6-6-document-summary-index","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s034","q":"Which retrieval mode does <code>doc_summary_index.as_query_engine()</code> use in LlamaIndex?","a":"<p>It uses the embedding-based retrieval by default. To use LLM-based selection you build <code>DocumentSummaryIndexLLMRetriever</code> yourself and wrap it in a <code>RetrieverQueryEngine</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0215","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-043-6-6-document-summary-index","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s034","q":"Why does Document Summary Index use <code>response_mode=\"tree_summarize\"</code> when it builds the summaries?","a":"<p>Documents are often longer than one prompt. <code>tree_summarize</code> summarizes pieces of the text, then summarizes those summaries recursively until a single result remains, which handles long input and suits summarization. Compare <code>refine</code>, which updates an answer chunk by chunk and is sequential.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0216","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-043-6-6-document-summary-index","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s034","q":"What are the costs and limits of a document summary index?","a":"<p>You pay for an LLM call per document at build time, summaries must be refreshed when documents change, and a summary can drop details that the question depends on. It suits corpora of many medium-sized documents with a clear topic each, and helps less for fact lookups inside one very long document.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0217","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-043-6-6-document-summary-index","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s034","q":"What does the Document Summary Index idea tell you about the best representation for retrieval versus synthesis?","a":"<p>The text that is best for finding documents (a compact summary) is not the text best for answering (the full chunks). The index separates the two: summaries for retrieval, linked chunks for synthesis. The same idea appears in sentence-window and parent-child retrieval.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0218","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-044-6-7-query-transformations","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s035","q":"What is query transformation in RAG and why do it before retrieval?","a":"<p>It rewrites, expands or decomposes the user's question before search. Users phrase questions vaguely or in several parts, so the raw query can be a poor match for the embeddings of the answer text. Common forms are query expansion or rewriting, sub-question decomposition and HyDE. The cost is extra LLM calls and latency.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0219","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-044-6-7-query-transformations","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s035","q":"How does multi-query expansion work, and what are its trade-offs?","a":"<p>An LLM, often with a few-shot prompt, writes several alternative phrasings of the question. Each is used for retrieval, and the results are merged, usually with reciprocal rank fusion. It improves recall for poorly phrased questions. Costs: more LLM and retrieval calls, and the alternatives can drift off topic and bring noise.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0220","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-044-6-7-query-transformations","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s035","q":"In a query-expansion prompt, why include few-shot examples and a placeholder for the number of queries?","a":"<p>Few-shot examples show the model the expected style and output shape (a numbered list of alternative questions), which makes the output predictable enough to parse by splitting on newlines. A placeholder such as <code>{num_queries}</code> lets you control the number of variants (cost versus recall) without rewriting the prompt.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0221","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-044-6-7-query-transformations","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s035","q":"When do you use <code>SubQuestionQueryEngine</code> in LlamaIndex?","a":"<p>When a question has several parts or spans several datasets, for example comparing two documents. The engine has an LLM split the question into sub-questions, each directed at a suitable <code>QueryEngineTool</code>, answers them (in parallel with <code>use_async=True</code>) and synthesizes one answer. For a single-fact question it only adds cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0222","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-044-6-7-query-transformations","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s035","q":"In LlamaIndex's <code>SubQuestionQueryEngine</code>, why must each <code>QueryEngineTool</code> carry a good name and description?","a":"<p>The LLM that creates the sub-questions picks the tool for each one based on those names and descriptions. Vague or overlapping descriptions lead to wrong routing and poor sub-answers. Write each description to say what the data covers and when to use it.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>tool = QueryEngineTool(\n    query_engine=index.as_query_engine(),\n    metadata=ToolMetadata(name=&quot;docs&quot;, description=&quot;Internal product documentation&quot;),\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0223","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-045-6-7-query-transformations","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s035","q":"What is HyDE (Hypothetical Document Embeddings) and why does it help retrieval?","a":"<p>HyDE asks the LLM to write a hypothetical answer to the question, embeds that text, and searches with that embedding. An answer looks more like the stored passages than a short question does, so its embedding sits closer to the relevant chunks. It needs no labels or fine-tuning (zero-shot, from the paper \"Precise Zero-Shot Dense Retrieval without Relevance Labels\").</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0224","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-045-6-7-query-transformations","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s035","q":"What is the drawback of HyDE, and why does it still work when the hypothetical answer is wrong?","a":"<p>The LLM may hallucinate, and the extra LLM call adds latency and cost. It can fail on queries about facts the LLM knows nothing about, where the invented answer points in the wrong direction. It often still works because the embedding only needs to land in the right topical neighborhood, and the real facts come from the retrieved documents.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0225","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-045-6-7-query-transformations","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s035","q":"In LlamaIndex, how do you use HyDE with an existing query engine, and what does <code>include_original=True</code> do?","a":"<p><code>TransformQueryEngine</code> wraps a base query engine and applies the transform to the query before retrieval. With <code>include_original=True</code> the original query's embedding is used together with the hypothetical document's, which protects against a bad hypothetical answer.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from llama_index.core.indices.query.query_transform import HyDEQueryTransform\nfrom llama_index.core.query_engine import TransformQueryEngine\n\nhyde = HyDEQueryTransform(include_original=True)\nhyde_engine = TransformQueryEngine(index.as_query_engine(), query_transform=hyde)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0226","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-045-6-7-query-transformations","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s035","q":"How do you choose among query expansion, sub-question decomposition and HyDE?","a":"<p>Use expansion when users phrase questions poorly and you want broader recall. Use sub-questions for multi-part or cross-document questions. Use HyDE for vague or broad queries where the question and the answer text embed far apart. They can be combined, but each adds latency, so measure the gain.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0227","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-045-6-7-query-transformations","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s035","q":"Which part of the pipeline does a query transformation change, and what stays the same?","a":"<p>Only the query that reaches the retriever changes. The index, chunking and the generator stay the same, and the final answer still uses the original user question in the prompt. That makes query transformations easy to add or remove when you test them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0228","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-046-7-advanced-rag-post-retrieval-other-tech","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s036","q":"In a LlamaIndex query, in what order do retrieval, reranking, post-processing and response generation happen?","a":"<p>The query engine retrieves nodes first, then reranking reorders them by relevance, then other node postprocessing filters or transforms them, and last the response synthesizer calls the LLM. Reranking and postprocessing both work on the retrieved nodes, so they plug in the same way, through <code>node_postprocessors</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0229","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-046-7-advanced-rag-post-retrieval-other-tech","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s036","q":"What is the difference between reranking and node post-processing in RAG?","a":"<p>Reranking changes only the order (and often the score) of retrieved nodes, using a stronger relevance model, and usually cuts the list to the best <code>top_n</code>. Other postprocessors change content or membership: drop low-score nodes, filter by keyword, replace text, compress or reorder for position. All run after retrieval and before the LLM.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0230","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-047-7-1-node-post-processing","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s037","q":"What is a node postprocessor in LlamaIndex, and where do you attach it?","a":"<p>It is a component (a <code>BaseNodePostProcessor</code>) that receives the retrieved nodes, filters or transforms them and returns them, between retrieval and response synthesis. You attach it to a query engine, for example <code>index.as_query_engine(node_postprocessors=[...])</code> or <code>RetrieverQueryEngine(retriever=..., node_postprocessors=[...])</code>, not to <code>as_retriever()</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0231","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-047-7-1-node-post-processing","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s037","q":"In LlamaIndex, how do you drop retrieved nodes with low similarity scores?","a":"<p><code>SimilarityPostprocessor</code> keeps only nodes whose score is at least <code>similarity_cutoff</code>. Retrieving 10 and filtering means the LLM sees fewer, more relevant chunks and can receive none if nothing passes. The right cutoff depends on the embedding model's score scale, so tune it on your data.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from llama_index.core.postprocessor import SimilarityPostprocessor\n\nquery_engine = index.as_query_engine(\n    similarity_top_k=10,\n    node_postprocessors=[SimilarityPostprocessor(similarity_cutoff=0.7)],\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0232","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-047-7-1-node-post-processing","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s037","q":"How does <code>KeywordNodePostprocessor</code> work in LlamaIndex and when would you use it?","a":"<p>It drops nodes that lack any <code>required_keywords</code> or contain any <code>exclude_keywords</code>, using spaCy-based matching. Use it for hard business rules (must mention a product, must not mention a competitor). It matches words, not meaning, so it is a blunt filter and the spaCy dependency has to be installed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0233","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-047-7-1-node-post-processing","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s037","q":"What is the \"lost in the middle\" problem, and which LlamaIndex postprocessor targets it?","a":"<p>Research (\"Lost in the Middle: How Language Models Use Long Contexts\") found that LLMs use facts at the beginning and end of a long prompt better than facts in the middle. <code>LongContextReorder</code> rearranges the retrieved nodes so the most relevant ones sit at the start and end and the weaker ones in the middle. It matters most when <code>top_k</code> is large.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0234","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-047-7-1-node-post-processing","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s037","q":"How does <code>LongContextReorder</code> order nodes in LlamaIndex?","a":"<p>It sorts nodes by score, then places them alternately: the best at the front, the second best at the end, and so on, so the lowest-ranked nodes end up in the middle. It adds no LLM call and does not remove anything.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0235","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-048-7-1-node-post-processing","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s037","q":"What does <code>SentenceEmbeddingOptimizer</code> do in LlamaIndex, and what are <code>percentile_cutoff</code> and <code>threshold_cutoff</code>?","a":"<p>It splits each retrieved node into sentences, embeds them, and keeps only the sentences most similar to the query, which shrinks the context sent to the LLM. <code>percentile_cutoff</code> keeps the top fraction of sentences (0.5 keeps the best half), and <code>threshold_cutoff</code> keeps sentences whose similarity exceeds a fixed value. It cuts token cost, but relevant sentences with weak similarity may be lost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0236","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-048-7-1-node-post-processing","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s037","q":"Compare <code>SimilarityPostprocessor</code> and <code>SentenceEmbeddingOptimizer</code>.","a":"<p><code>SimilarityPostprocessor</code> removes whole nodes that score under a cutoff. <code>SentenceEmbeddingOptimizer</code> trims inside nodes, keeping the relevant sentences of each. Use the first to discard irrelevant chunks and the second to reduce the size of long chunks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0237","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-048-7-1-node-post-processing","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s037","q":"How would you debug which nodes reached the LLM after postprocessing in LlamaIndex?","a":"<p>Inspect <code>response.source_nodes</code>, which holds the nodes (with scores) used to build the answer after postprocessing. Compare it with <code>retriever.retrieve(query)</code> to see what each postprocessor removed. Printing each node's text with <code>get_content(metadata_mode=\"llm\")</code> shows what the LLM was actually given.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0238","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-048-7-1-node-post-processing","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s037","q":"Several postprocessors are in the <code>node_postprocessors</code> list. In what order do they run, and why does it matter?","a":"<p>They run in list order, each receiving the previous one's output. For example, filter by similarity first, then reorder, then compress, so you do not spend work on nodes that will be removed. Reordering before filtering or reranking after compression produces different results.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0239","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-049-7-2-re-ranking","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s038","q":"What does a reranker do in a RAG pipeline, and why is it more accurate than the first-stage retriever?","a":"<p>A reranker rescores the retrieved candidates against the query and reorders them, keeping the best <code>top_n</code>. First-stage retrieval compares precomputed embeddings (bi-encoder), which is fast but coarse. A reranker such as a cross-encoder reads the query and each passage together, which is more accurate but too slow to run over the whole corpus.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0240","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-049-7-2-re-ranking","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s038","q":"How should you set <code>similarity_top_k</code> and <code>top_n</code> when using Cohere Rerank with a LlamaIndex query engine?","a":"<p>Set <code>similarity_top_k</code> high (over-retrieve, such as 15) so recall is good, and <code>top_n</code> low (such as 5) for the chunks that go to the LLM. The reranker then picks the best ones from a wide pool. If <code>top_k</code> equals <code>top_n</code>, reranking only reorders and cannot rescue a relevant chunk ranked low.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from llama_index.postprocessor.cohere_rerank import CohereRerank\n\nreranker = CohereRerank(top_n=5, api_key=cohere_api_key)\nquery_engine = index.as_query_engine(similarity_top_k=15, node_postprocessors=[reranker])</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0241","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-049-7-2-re-ranking","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s038","q":"What trade-offs come with adding a reranker?","a":"<p>It improves precision and the ordering of context, but adds latency (a model call over every candidate), cost for a hosted API, and a dependency. Candidate count drives that cost, so keep the pool as small as recall allows.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0242","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-049-7-2-re-ranking","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s038","q":"What are the options for a reranker in LlamaIndex besides Cohere?","a":"<p>Other <code>node_postprocessors</code> that follow the same pattern include the ColBERT reranker (<code>llama-index-postprocessor-colbert-rerank</code>), the Flag Embedding reranker (<code>llama-index-postprocessor-flag-embedding-reranker</code>, local BGE rerank models), and LLM-based rerankers. Local models avoid sending data to an API and per-call fees but need compute.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0243","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-049-7-2-re-ranking","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s038","q":"When is reranking likely not worth it?","a":"<p>When the first-stage retrieval is already precise (small corpora, strong hybrid search), when latency or cost budgets are tight, or when the bottleneck is elsewhere (poor chunking, missing documents). Measure on an evaluation set before adding it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0244","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-050-7-3-flare-forward-looking-active-retriev","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s039","q":"What is FLARE and what limitation of standard RAG does it address?","a":"<p>FLARE (Forward-Looking Active REtrieval, from the paper \"Active Retrieval Augmented Generation\") retrieves repeatedly while the answer is written, deciding when and what to retrieve. Standard RAG retrieves once before generation, which is not enough for long answers whose needs change as the text proceeds.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0245","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-050-7-3-flare-forward-looking-active-retriev","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s039","q":"How does the FLARE loop work?","a":"<p>The LLM drafts the next sentence. If all its tokens have high probability, it keeps the sentence. If some tokens have low confidence, the draft (or a version with the uncertain parts masked) is used as a search query, documents are retrieved, and the sentence is written again with them. This repeats until the answer is complete or a maximum number of iterations is reached.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0246","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-050-7-3-flare-forward-looking-active-retriev","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s039","q":"Why does FLARE retrieve only when the model is uncertain, rather than at every step?","a":"<p>Retrieval costs time and tokens and can add noise when the model already knows the facts. Token confidence is a signal that the model lacks knowledge. So retrieval happens only where it is likely to help, which reduces calls compared with always retrieving.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0247","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-050-7-3-flare-forward-looking-active-retriev","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s039","q":"In LlamaIndex, how do you set up FLARE, and what does <code>max_iterations</code> do?","a":"<p><code>FLAREInstructQueryEngine</code> wraps a base query engine, which it calls for retrieval whenever the LLM writes a search query. <code>max_iterations</code> limits the loop of generate-and-retrieve steps so it cannot run forever or cost too much. (In LlamaIndex 0.10+, this is the FLARE Instruct variant; it relies on the LLM following a prompt format.)</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from llama_index.core.query_engine import FLAREInstructQueryEngine\n\nflare_engine = FLAREInstructQueryEngine(\n    query_engine=index.as_query_engine(similarity_top_k=3),\n    max_iterations=7,\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0248","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-050-7-3-flare-forward-looking-active-retriev","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s039","q":"What is the difference between the FLARE Instruct and FLARE Direct variants?","a":"<p>FLARE Instruct prompts the LLM to insert search queries itself when it needs information, in a format the engine can parse. FLARE Direct uses the LLM's own drafted sentence as the search query when it contains low-confidence tokens. Direct needs access to token probabilities, which many hosted APIs expose only partly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0249","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-050-7-3-flare-forward-looking-active-retriev","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s039","q":"When would you pick FLARE over standard RAG, and what do you give up?","a":"<p>Pick it for long-form, knowledge-intensive output (reports, multi-part explanations) where facts needed later are not known from the first question. You give up simplicity: many more LLM calls, higher latency and cost, and sensitivity to prompt formats. For short factual answers standard RAG is enough.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0250","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-051-7-4-prompt-compression-longllmlingua","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s040","q":"What is prompt compression in RAG and why use it?","a":"<p>It shortens the retrieved context while keeping the information relevant to the question. This lowers token cost and latency, fits more evidence within the context window, and reduces the \"lost in the middle\" effect. The risk is dropping a detail the answer needs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0251","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-051-7-4-prompt-compression-longllmlingua","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s040","q":"How does LongLLMLingua decide what to remove?","a":"<p>It uses a small language model to measure perplexity. Question-aware coarse-grained compression ranks whole documents by how relevant they are to the question. Fine-grained compression keeps tokens that matter, using contrastive perplexity with and without the question. Adaptive granular control gives more important documents a lower compression ratio, and subsequence recovery maps the answer back to the original text.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0252","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-051-7-4-prompt-compression-longllmlingua","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s040","q":"In LlamaIndex, how do you use LongLLMLingua, and what does <code>target_token</code> do?","a":"<p><code>LongLLMLinguaPostprocessor</code> is a node postprocessor, so it is attached through <code>node_postprocessors</code>. <code>target_token</code> is the token budget the compressed context should reach. <code>instruction_str</code> is the task instruction the compression is conditioned on. It loads a local language model (in recent versions a Llama-2 7B model on a GPU by default), which is a notable infrastructure cost.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from llama_index.postprocessor.longllmlingua import LongLLMLinguaPostprocessor\n\ncompressor = LongLLMLinguaPostprocessor(\n    instruction_str=&quot;Given the context, please answer the final question&quot;,\n    target_token=300,\n    rank_method=&quot;longllmlingua&quot;,\n)\nquery_engine = index.as_query_engine(node_postprocessors=[compressor])</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0253","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-051-7-4-prompt-compression-longllmlingua","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s040","q":"What are the trade-offs of prompt compression compared with reranking and filtering?","a":"<p>Compression cuts tokens inside the chunks, so it can fit more evidence into a budget, but it can remove needed words, makes the text less readable, and needs an extra model (often a GPU). Reranking and filtering keep text intact and are cheaper to run. Use compression when context length or cost is the bottleneck.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0254","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-052-7-5-self-correcting-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s041","q":"What is self-correcting RAG, and how does it work in LlamaIndex?","a":"<p>The system evaluates its own answer and retries when the answer fails. A query engine such as <code>RetryQueryEngine</code> runs the base engine, scores the response with an evaluator, and if it fails it transforms the query using the evaluation feedback and queries again, up to <code>max_retries</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0255","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-052-7-5-self-correcting-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s041","q":"How do <code>RetryQueryEngine</code>, <code>RetrySourceQueryEngine</code> and <code>RetryGuidelineQueryEngine</code> differ in LlamaIndex?","a":"<p><code>RetryQueryEngine</code> uses a <code>RelevancyEvaluator</code> and retries with a transformed query. <code>RetrySourceQueryEngine</code> evaluates each retrieved source node, keeps only the passing nodes, builds a new index from them and retries on it. <code>RetryGuidelineQueryEngine</code> uses a <code>GuidelineEvaluator</code> with your own guidelines and rewrites the query from the feedback.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0256","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-052-7-5-self-correcting-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s041","q":"Why is a <code>GuidelineEvaluator</code> useful in a retry loop?","a":"<p>It judges the response against rules you write in natural language (tone, length, citing context, policy), and returns a pass/fail with feedback text. That feedback drives the next query. It lets you enforce business or style requirements, not just relevance.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from llama_index.core.evaluation import GuidelineEvaluator\n\nguideline_eval = GuidelineEvaluator(\n    guidelines=&quot;The response should not be overly long.\\nThe response should cite its context.&quot;\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0257","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-052-7-5-self-correcting-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s041","q":"What are the risks of self-correcting RAG in production?","a":"<p>Each retry adds an evaluation call and another query, so cost and latency multiply, with a worst case of about <code>max_retries</code> times the normal cost. The evaluator is an LLM and can be wrong, so it may reject good answers or accept bad ones. Cap retries, log them, and use it where answer quality matters more than speed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0258","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-052-7-5-self-correcting-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s041","q":"How does the retry approach compare with improving retrieval beforehand (better chunking, reranking)?","a":"<p>Retry is a safety net that repairs failures at query time, at the price of higher cost per failure. Better retrieval prevents failures for every query at index time or with one-off cost. First fix systematic retrieval problems, then add retries for the remaining cases.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0259","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-053-8-modular-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s042","q":"What does \"Modular RAG\" mean as a design approach?","a":"<p>It treats a RAG system as a set of composable modules, such as retrievers (vector, keyword, hybrid), routers, query transformations, rerankers, memory and agents, connected into a pipeline that can branch or loop. You can swap, add or route between modules per query. The benefit is flexibility and the ability to tune each part. The cost is complexity and more to evaluate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0260","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-053-8-modular-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s042","q":"How do you decide whether a RAG system needs modular components such as routing or agents instead of a single vector search?","a":"<p>Check the failure modes on an evaluation set. If questions come from different data types or need different strategies (summaries, keyword lookups, structured filters, multi-step reasoning), a single retriever will fail on some of them and routing or agents will help. If one strategy works on all, extra modules only add latency and failure points.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0261","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-054-8-1-hybrid-retrieval","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s043","q":"What is hybrid retrieval and why combine vector search and keyword search?","a":"<p>Hybrid retrieval combines dense vector search (semantic similarity) with keyword search such as BM25 (exact term matches). Vector search finds paraphrases but can miss exact names, codes or rare terms. BM25 finds exact terms but misses synonyms. Combining them raises recall and robustness. It needs two retrieval paths and a way to merge scores.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0262","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-054-8-1-hybrid-retrieval","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s043","q":"When would pure vector search fail where BM25 succeeds, and the reverse?","a":"<p>Vector search can fail on product codes, error IDs, names and rare jargon, where the exact string matters and embeddings blur it. BM25 fails when the query uses different words from the document (\"car\" versus \"automobile\") or is a vague natural-language question.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0263","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-054-8-1-hybrid-retrieval","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s043","q":"How does BM25 rank documents?","a":"<p>BM25 scores a document by the query terms it contains, with each term weighted by how rare it is across the corpus (inverse document frequency) and by its frequency in the document, with saturation and a correction for document length. It is a sparse lexical method with no training and no embeddings.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0264","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-054-8-1-hybrid-retrieval","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s043","q":"In LlamaIndex, why does a BM25 retriever need a docstore?","a":"<p>BM25 works on the raw text of the nodes and builds its term statistics from them, and the vector store holds embeddings, not the text-index structure BM25 needs. The retriever reads the nodes from the docstore. So you must put the nodes in a docstore (for example a <code>SimpleDocumentStore</code> in the <code>StorageContext</code>) when you build the index. <code>BM25Retriever</code> comes from the separate <code>llama-index-retrievers-bm25</code> package.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>bm25_retriever = BM25Retriever.from_defaults(docstore=index.docstore, similarity_top_k=2)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0265","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-054-8-1-hybrid-retrieval","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s043","q":"What do sparse and dense vectors mean in hybrid search?","a":"<p>A dense vector has many (hundreds or thousands) real-valued dimensions that capture meaning. A sparse vector has a huge dimension (one per vocabulary term) with mostly zeros, so it captures which terms occur and how important they are (BM25 or SPLADE-style). Dense is good for meaning, sparse for exact terms, and some vector databases store both in one collection.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0266","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-055-8-1-hybrid-retrieval","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s043","q":"In LlamaIndex, which <code>vector_store_query_mode</code> values exist for vector stores, and what does each do?","a":"<p><code>default</code> is pure dense search. <code>hybrid</code> combines dense and keyword/sparse search weighted by <code>alpha</code>. <code>semantic_hybrid</code> adds a semantic reranking step to hybrid. <code>sparse</code> uses sparse vectors only. <code>text_search</code> uses exact keyword matching. Which modes work depends on the vector store integration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0267","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-055-8-1-hybrid-retrieval","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s043","q":"In LlamaIndex hybrid mode, what does <code>alpha</code> control?","a":"<p><code>alpha</code> weights the two searches: 0 means keyword-only and 1 means vector-only. A value of 0.25 leans toward keyword matching. Lower it when exact terms matter (codes, names) and raise it when queries are natural-language questions. Tune it on an evaluation set.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>retriever = index.as_retriever(\n    vector_store_query_mode=&quot;hybrid&quot;, alpha=0.25, similarity_top_k=3\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0268","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-055-8-1-hybrid-retrieval","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s043","q":"What must you set up in a vector store such as Qdrant to use hybrid mode in LlamaIndex?","a":"<p>The vector store must hold both dense and sparse vectors, which in the LlamaIndex Qdrant integration is switched on with <code>enable_hybrid=True</code> when the store is created. For sparse vectors you also need a sparse embedding model (for example a SPLADE model through FastEmbed), and you must set it at both ingest and query time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0269","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-055-8-1-hybrid-retrieval","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s043","q":"What is the difference between <code>similarity_top_k</code> and <code>sparse_top_k</code> for hybrid queries in LlamaIndex?","a":"<p><code>sparse_top_k</code> is how many nodes the sparse query fetches (and the dense query fetches <code>similarity_top_k</code>), before they are merged. The final result list after fusion is limited by <code>similarity_top_k</code> (or <code>hybrid_top_k</code> in some modes). Fetch more per search than you finally need so the merge has candidates to work with.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0270","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-055-8-1-hybrid-retrieval","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s043","q":"What is Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF), and why is it popular for combining retrievers?","a":"<p>RRF merges ranked lists using only ranks. Each document gets the sum over lists of <code>1 / (k + rank)</code> (k is a smoothing constant, commonly 60), and documents are sorted by that total. Items ranked high by several retrievers rise to the top. It needs no score normalization, which matters because BM25 scores and cosine similarities are on different scales.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0271","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-055-8-1-hybrid-retrieval","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s043","q":"Show how RRF scores a document that is ranked 1st by one retriever and 3rd by another, with k=60.","a":"<p>It prints <code>0.0323</code>: 1/61 is about 0.01639 and 1/63 about 0.01587, which sum to about 0.03226. A document seen by only one retriever gets just one term, so it ranks lower than documents that appear in both lists at similar positions.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>k = 60\nscore = 1 / (k + 1) + 1 / (k + 3)\nprint(round(score, 4))</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0272","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-056-8-1-hybrid-retrieval","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s043","q":"What does <code>QueryFusionRetriever</code> do in LlamaIndex?","a":"<p>It takes several retrievers (for example vector and BM25), optionally has an LLM write several variations of the query (<code>num_queries</code>), runs each query on every retriever, and fuses the result lists into one ranked list with a chosen <code>mode</code> such as <code>reciprocal_rerank</code>. It returns the top <code>similarity_top_k</code> nodes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0273","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-056-8-1-hybrid-retrieval","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s043","q":"How do you stop <code>QueryFusionRetriever</code> from calling the LLM to rewrite the query?","a":"<p>Set <code>num_queries=1</code>. The retriever then uses only the original question and skips query generation, so you get a pure fusion of the retrievers without extra LLM latency or cost.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>retriever = QueryFusionRetriever(\n    [vector_retriever, bm25_retriever],\n    similarity_top_k=5,\n    num_queries=1,\n    mode=&quot;reciprocal_rerank&quot;,\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0274","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-056-8-1-hybrid-retrieval","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s043","q":"How do the fusion modes <code>reciprocal_rerank</code>, <code>relative_score</code>, <code>dist_based_score</code> and <code>simple</code> of <code>QueryFusionRetriever</code> differ?","a":"<p><code>reciprocal_rerank</code> uses RRF on ranks only and is the safe general default. <code>relative_score</code> min-max scales each retriever's scores to 0-1 and combines them with weights. <code>dist_based_score</code> scales by mean and standard deviation, so it is less sensitive to outliers. <code>simple</code> just reorders by the original scores, which is only valid if the scores are comparable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0275","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-056-8-1-hybrid-retrieval","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s043","q":"Why use <code>use_async=True</code> in <code>QueryFusionRetriever</code>?","a":"<p>The fusion retriever runs many retrievals (queries times retrievers), and these are independent. With async they run concurrently, so latency is close to the slowest call rather than the sum. It does not reduce cost, and rate limits of the providers still apply.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0276","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-056-8-1-hybrid-retrieval","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s043","q":"Why is <code>similarity_top_k</code> of each inner retriever often smaller than the <code>similarity_top_k</code> of the fusion retriever?","a":"<p>Each retriever contributes its own candidates for each query, and the fusion step merges them, so the union can be larger than any single list. The fusion <code>similarity_top_k</code> sets the final size after merging. Keep inner values large enough that a good result found by only one retriever is still in the pool.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0277","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-056-8-1-hybrid-retrieval","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s043","q":"Why is BM25 plus vector search with RRF often called a cheap upgrade for RAG?","a":"<p>It needs no training, no extra model and almost no extra compute, and RRF needs no tuning. BM25 adds exact matching where embeddings are weak and vector search adds semantics where keywords are weak. Typical gains are better recall and robustness. The cost is maintaining a second index.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0278","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-057-8-2-agentic-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s044","q":"What is agentic RAG?","a":"<p>RAG where an agent controls retrieval: it chooses whether to retrieve, which index or tool to use, how to build filters and query, and whether to retrieve again. That helps with multi-step questions and with mixed datasets. Compared with a fixed pipeline it is more flexible but harder to test and control.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0279","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-057-8-2-agentic-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s044","q":"What is auto-retrieval, and how does an LLM turn a question into a filtered vector search?","a":"<p>Auto-retrieval has the LLM convert a natural-language question into a search query plus metadata filters (for example author equals X). It fills a function schema with those values, and the function runs a filtered retrieval against the vector store. It needs a description of the available metadata so the LLM knows which fields exist and how to use them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0280","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-057-8-2-agentic-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s044","q":"In LlamaIndex, what is the role of <code>VectorStoreInfo</code> and <code>MetadataInfo</code> in auto-retrieval?","a":"<p>They describe the content and the schema of metadata to the LLM: field names, types and descriptions. The LLM uses the descriptions to decide which filter to apply for a question, so vague descriptions produce wrong filters. They are usually serialized into the tool description.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from llama_index.core.vector_stores.types import VectorStoreInfo, MetadataInfo\n\nvector_store_info = VectorStoreInfo(\n    content_info=&quot;Technical articles about databases&quot;,\n    metadata_info=[MetadataInfo(name=&quot;author&quot;, type=&quot;str&quot;, description=&quot;Name of the article&#x27;s author&quot;)],\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0281","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-057-8-2-agentic-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s044","q":"Why define a Pydantic model and pass it as <code>fn_schema</code> to a LlamaIndex <code>FunctionTool</code>?","a":"<p><code>fn_schema</code> gives the LLM an explicit, typed argument schema (names, types, descriptions) for the tool call, so it produces structured arguments, such as lists of filter keys and values, that validate. Without it the schema is inferred from the function signature, which loses the field descriptions that guide the LLM.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from typing import List\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field\n\nclass AutoRetrieveModel(BaseModel):\n    query: str = Field(..., description=&quot;The search query&quot;)\n    filter_key_list: List[str] = Field(..., description=&quot;Metadata field names to filter on&quot;)\n    filter_value_list: List[str] = Field(..., description=&quot;Values matching the field names&quot;)\n\n# FunctionTool.from_defaults(fn=auto_retrieve_fn, name=&quot;search&quot;, description=desc, fn_schema=AutoRetrieveModel)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0282","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-058-8-2-agentic-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s044","q":"In LlamaIndex, how does the tool description of a <code>FunctionTool</code> affect the agent's behavior?","a":"<p>The agent chooses tools by reading their names and descriptions, so the description must say what the tool does, when to use it, and what the arguments mean. A missing or vague description leads to the tool not being called, or being called with wrong arguments. For retrieval tools it is common to embed the metadata schema in the description.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0283","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-058-8-2-agentic-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s044","q":"In an auto-retrieval tool, how do you apply the LLM-chosen filters with LlamaIndex classes?","a":"<p>Build one <code>MetadataFilter</code> per key and value pair, wrap them in <code>MetadataFilters</code>, and pass them to the retriever as <code>filters</code>. The vector store applies them during the search. <code>FilterOperator.CONTAINS</code> matches partial text, while <code>EQ</code> needs an exact match, so the operator choice changes recall. Not every vector store supports every operator.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from llama_index.core.vector_stores.types import MetadataFilter, FilterOperator, MetadataFilters\n\nfilters = MetadataFilters(filters=[\n    MetadataFilter(key=&quot;author&quot;, value=&quot;Seneca&quot;, operator=FilterOperator.CONTAINS)\n])\nretriever = index.as_retriever(filters=filters, similarity_top_k=3)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0284","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-058-8-2-agentic-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s044","q":"In LlamaIndex 0.10 to 0.11, what are the roles of <code>AgentRunner</code> and <code>AgentWorker</code>?","a":"<p><code>AgentRunner</code> is the orchestrator: it holds the conversation memory and state, creates tasks and runs them step by step, and exposes <code>chat</code>. <code>AgentWorker</code> (for example <code>FunctionCallingAgentWorker</code>) decides how a single step is executed and holds no state itself. This split lets you control a run step by step and plug in different reasoning strategies. Newer LlamaIndex versions move agents to workflow-based classes, so check the current docs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0285","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-058-8-2-agentic-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s044","q":"What are the risks of an LLM that chooses its own metadata filters?","a":"<p>It may invent field values that do not exist, pick the wrong field, or filter too narrowly, which returns zero results even though relevant text exists. Mitigations: precise field descriptions, a fallback to unfiltered search when nothing is returned, validating values against known ones, and testing with an evaluation set of questions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0286","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-058-8-2-agentic-rag","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s044","q":"When is an agent the wrong choice for a RAG application?","a":"<p>When questions are of one type and a fixed pipeline answers them well. Agents add LLM calls, cost, latency and nondeterminism, and are harder to debug and evaluate. Start with a plain query engine and move to an agent when queries need tool selection, filters, or multiple steps.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0287","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-059-8-3-ensemble-retrieval","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s045","q":"What is ensemble retrieval in RAG, and why would you use it?","a":"<p>Several retrieval strategies run on the same question (for example indexes with different chunk sizes, or different retrievers), their results are merged and then reranked. It increases recall and lets you compare strategies, because different configurations retrieve different relevant chunks. The cost is multiple indexes and retrieval calls plus a reranking step.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0288","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-059-8-3-ensemble-retrieval","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s045","q":"In LlamaIndex, how do you build an ensemble over several chunk-size indexes, and what do <code>IndexNode</code> and <code>SummaryIndex</code> do here?","a":"<p>Build a vector index for each chunk size. Wrap each index's retriever behind an <code>IndexNode</code> whose <code>index_id</code> names it, and put these nodes in a <code>SummaryIndex</code>. A <code>RecursiveRetriever</code> uses the summary index retriever as the root, which returns every <code>IndexNode</code>, and then follows each reference to the corresponding retriever. The result is one merged list of nodes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0289","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-059-8-3-ensemble-retrieval","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s045","q":"Why is a <code>SummaryIndex</code> used as the root of the ensemble in LlamaIndex?","a":"<p>A <code>SummaryIndex</code> retriever returns all its nodes by default (no similarity filtering), so every <code>IndexNode</code> and therefore every underlying retriever is called for each query. A vector root would select only some of them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0290","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-059-8-3-ensemble-retrieval","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s045","q":"Why add a reranker after merging the results of an ensemble retriever?","a":"<p>Merged lists contain duplicates and chunks with scores from different indexes that cannot be compared directly. A reranker scores all candidates against the query with the same model and orders them consistently, then you keep the best <code>top_n</code>. Without it, the order depends on how the lists were concatenated.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0291","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-059-8-3-ensemble-retrieval","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s045","q":"In an ensemble over chunk sizes, why store <code>chunk_size</code> in node metadata and add it to the excluded embedding and LLM metadata keys?","a":"<p>The metadata lets you trace which strategy produced each retrieved node and so evaluate strategies later. Excluding it from embedding and LLM text keeps it from changing the vectors or confusing the LLM. It stays in the node for analysis.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>node.metadata[&quot;chunk_size&quot;] = chunk_size\nnode.excluded_embed_metadata_keys = [&quot;chunk_size&quot;]\nnode.excluded_llm_metadata_keys = [&quot;chunk_size&quot;]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0292","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-059-8-3-ensemble-retrieval","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s045","q":"In an ensemble over several chunk sizes, how can you use the reciprocal rank of the first hit to find which chunk size contributes best?","a":"<p>After reranking, find the position of the first node produced by each chunk size and compute 1/position. A higher value means that strategy's best chunk ranks earlier in the final list. Averaging over many questions shows which chunk size consistently gives the most relevant context. One question is too noisy to decide.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0293","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-060-8-4-ensemble-query-engine","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s046","q":"What is an ensemble query engine, and how does it differ from ensemble retrieval?","a":"<p>An ensemble query engine runs several complete query engines (for example vector, keyword table and knowledge graph) and combines their answers, with a router LLM choosing which to use and a summarizer merging the results. Ensemble retrieval merges retrieved nodes from several retrievers before one generation step. The first mixes answers, the second mixes evidence.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0294","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-060-8-4-ensemble-query-engine","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s046","q":"What does <code>RouterQueryEngine</code> do in LlamaIndex, and what are its main parts?","a":"<p>It routes each query to one or more query engines. It needs a selector (an LLM or Pydantic-based chooser), a list of <code>QueryEngineTool</code>s with descriptions that say what each engine is for, and, when several tools may be used, a summarizer to combine their answers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0295","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-060-8-4-ensemble-query-engine","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s046","q":"In LlamaIndex, what is the difference between <code>LLMSingleSelector</code> and <code>LLMMultiSelector</code>?","a":"<p><code>LLMSingleSelector</code> selects exactly one tool, so each question goes to one engine. <code>LLMMultiSelector</code> lets the LLM choose several tools, runs all of them and combines the answers with the summarizer. Use multi when a question may benefit from different strategies, at the cost of more calls.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0296","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-060-8-4-ensemble-query-engine","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s046","q":"In <code>RouterQueryEngine</code>, which text does the selector LLM use to choose a tool, and what does that mean for how you write it?","a":"<p>It uses the tool <code>description</code>. The LLM reads all descriptions and the query and picks the best match, so they must be specific and non-overlapping, saying what data or kind of question each engine suits. Poor descriptions cause wrong routing.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>vector_tool = QueryEngineTool.from_defaults(\n    query_engine=vector_query_engine,\n    description=&quot;Useful for answering fully-formed questions from a user.&quot;,\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0297","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-061-8-4-ensemble-query-engine","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s046","q":"When would a keyword table index engine beat a vector engine, and how does a router help?","a":"<p>A keyword table index matches on extracted keywords, so it does well on keyword-style or incomplete inputs (\"refund policy\") and exact terms. A vector engine is better for fully formed natural-language questions. A router with clear tool descriptions sends each input to the suitable engine, or to both.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0298","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-061-8-4-ensemble-query-engine","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s046","q":"What is the role of <code>TreeSummarize</code> as the summarizer of a <code>RouterQueryEngine</code> in LlamaIndex?","a":"<p>When several engines answer, the summarizer merges their answers into one. <code>TreeSummarize</code> does this by summarizing in a tree: it combines the answers in groups and summarizes recursively, so it works even when the combined text exceeds one prompt. The template can instruct the LLM to weigh relevance scores and to say so when the answer is not in the context.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>tree_summarize = TreeSummarize(summary_template=PromptTemplate(template_text))\nquery_engine = RouterQueryEngine(\n    selector=LLMMultiSelector.from_defaults(),\n    query_engine_tools=[keyword_tool, vector_tool],\n    summarizer=tree_summarize,\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0299","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-061-8-4-ensemble-query-engine","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s046","q":"Why might you ask each query engine in an ensemble to report a relevance score with its answer?","a":"<p>The final summarizer LLM can then weigh the answers: trust the high-scoring one and discount the low-scoring one. It is self-reported by an LLM, so it is a rough signal that can be miscalibrated. It is a cheap alternative to a separate judge call.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0300","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-061-8-4-ensemble-query-engine","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s046","q":"What are the costs of an ensemble query engine compared with a single vector query engine?","a":"<p>Each question triggers a routing call, one or several engine calls, and a summarization call, so latency and cost are higher, and there are more failure points. Run it only when a single engine fails on part of your questions, and measure the improvement on an evaluation set.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0301","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-061-8-4-ensemble-query-engine","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s046","q":"Why use <code>aquery</code> and <code>await</code> with a <code>RouterQueryEngine</code> that has several tools?","a":"<p>The async version runs the selected engines concurrently, which cuts latency to about the slowest engine. In a notebook or async application you <code>await query_engine.aquery(...)</code>. The synchronous <code>query</code> runs them one by one.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0302","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-062-llamaindex-evaluation-module","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s047","q":"What are the two kinds of evaluation in RAG, and how does LlamaIndex support them?","a":"<p>Component-wise evaluation tests one part, such as the retriever, on its own. End-to-end evaluation tests the full system output on metrics like faithfulness and relevancy. LlamaIndex has an evaluation module with evaluators for both, and most of the response metrics are LLM-as-a-judge calls.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0303","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-062-llamaindex-evaluation-module","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s047","q":"In LlamaIndex, which evaluator classes exist for response quality, and what do they check?","a":"<p><code>FaithfulnessEvaluator</code> checks that the answer is supported by the retrieved context (hallucination). <code>RelevancyEvaluator</code> checks that the response and context are relevant to the query. <code>CorrectnessEvaluator</code> compares the answer with a reference answer. <code>GuidelineEvaluator</code> checks against custom rules. <code>BatchEvalRunner</code> runs several evaluators over many queries (in LlamaIndex 0.10+).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0304","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-062-llamaindex-evaluation-module","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s047","q":"In LlamaIndex, how do you score one answer with an evaluator, and what does the result contain?","a":"<p><code>evaluate_response</code> takes the query and the <code>Response</code> object (which carries the source nodes) and asks the judge LLM whether the answer is supported by that context. The returned <code>EvaluationResult</code> has <code>passing</code> (bool), <code>score</code> (a float, 1.0 for pass with the faithfulness evaluator), <code>feedback</code> (the judge's text) and the query and response. Pass a separate <code>llm</code> as judge so you can use a stronger model than the one that answered.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from llama_index.core.evaluation import FaithfulnessEvaluator\n\nevaluator = FaithfulnessEvaluator(llm=judge_llm)\nresponse = query_engine.query(&quot;What is the refund policy?&quot;)\nresult = evaluator.evaluate_response(query=&quot;What is the refund policy?&quot;, response=response)\nprint(result.passing, result.score, result.feedback)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0305","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-063-comparative-analysis-of-rag-techniques-a","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s048","q":"What did the ARAGOG study compare, and which approach had the best retrieval precision?","a":"<p>ARAGOG (Advanced RAG Output Grading) compared advanced RAG techniques such as sentence-window retrieval, document summary index, HyDE, multi-query, MMR, Cohere reranker and LLM reranker, built with LlamaIndex, over a set of arXiv papers. HyDE combined with LLM reranking gave the best retrieval precision and answer similarity. Treat the ranking as evidence for that setup, and verify it on your data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0306","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-063-comparative-analysis-of-rag-techniques-a","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s048","q":"Why did ARAGOG run each technique several times and use statistical tests?","a":"<p>LLM outputs vary between runs, so one run can make a technique look better or worse by chance. Repeated runs plus a significance test (ANOVA, then Tukey HSD for pairwise comparisons) show whether differences are real. Without this, small gaps between techniques are noise.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0307","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-063-comparative-analysis-of-rag-techniques-a","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s048","q":"According to ARAGOG-style findings, why should you prioritize retrieval precision in a RAG system?","a":"<p>The model can only be faithful to what it receives, so precise context leads to grounded answers, and irrelevant context leads to distraction or hallucination. Answer similarity also depends on the LLM and the prompt, so improving retrieval alone does not guarantee better answers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"rag-llamaindex-f0308","card":"q-rag-llamaindex-063-comparative-analysis-of-rag-techniques-a","sec":"rag-llamaindex-s048","q":"Based on the ARAGOG findings, what is a sensible baseline for an advanced RAG pipeline?","a":"<p>Start with hybrid retrieval and reranking, add a query transformation such as HyDE, and use sentence-window retrieval where precision is critical. Then verify the combination on your own evaluation set, since results depend on documents, models and questions.</p>","hand":true}];
